WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.100
- Welcome everyone. My
name is Priya Natarajan

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.780
and I'm the current director

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.660
of the Franke Program in
Science and Humanities.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.630
I'm delighted to invite
you all to this evening's

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.690
wonderful event as part of our
distinguished author series.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:23.160
First, I would like to start
by recognizing our benefactors,

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.500
Mr. and Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.750
and their support of the Franke Program,

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.850
Yale's flagship interdisciplinary program,

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.560
as well as multiple other initiatives

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.450
at Yale that they support.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.620
So I wanna remind you
that as Ty mentioned,

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.380
we have a wonderful reception
upstairs after the talk.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:41.610
So please do stay for that.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.103
It's in the student lounge room 131.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.860
And since we are recording this talk,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.870
I'm required to read this out to you

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.700
that Yale University's photographing

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.260
and videotaping this event.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.450
There's a possibility
that you may be included

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:57.360
in these visuals by being
present during these activities.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.550
You consent to the use of your appearance,

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:02.100
likeness and voice by Yale University

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.950
for standard non-profit,
educational, news-related,

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.203
promotional and or editorial purposes.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.000
So today we're in for a real
treat from one of our own

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.550
distinguished faculty,
professor Beverly Gage,

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.220
who is a professor of
history and American studies.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.970
Her research interests lie
in 20th century U.S. history,

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.830
government and political development,

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.840
and ideological movements.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.080
She teaches a wide variety
of extremely popular courses

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.450
that focus on American
politics, government,

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.400
and social movements.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.227
Today she will be talking
to us about her new book,

00:01:35.227 --> 00:01:37.177
"G-Man J. Edgar Hoover

00:01:37.177 --> 00:01:39.267
"and the Making of the American Century."

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.190
This book has done incredibly well.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:47.820
It's completely unsurprising
if you have opened up and read,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.550
started even reading the
first couple of pages.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.460
The writing is brilliant,
the analysis is incisive.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.230
I highly recommend getting a copy

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.240
if you don't already have it.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.223
So G-Man received the,

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:03.750
Beverly received the 2022 Bancroft Prize

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:04.890
in American History,

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.740
and was named a best book of 2022

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.927
by "Washington Post" in
their list of 10 best books,

00:02:10.927 --> 00:02:13.267
"The Atlantic," in their 10 best books,

00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:16.230
"Publishers Weekly,"
in their 10 best books.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.120
The list is very long, but
I'll read some of them,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.010
just to show how spectacularly
successful Beverly

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:27.540
has managed to reengage us
with this very complicated man

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.040
all over again.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.490
So "The New Yorker" in
their 24 essential reads,

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.860
the "New York Times" in
their 100 notable books,

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.770
and in the "Smithsonian" in
their 10 best history books.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.477
She's also the author of "The
Day Wall Street Exploded,

00:02:40.477 --> 00:02:43.260
"A Story of America in
its First Age of Terror,"

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.180
which examined the history of terrorism

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.460
in the late 19th and 20th centuries,

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:50.610
focusing on the 1920 Wall Street bombing.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.530
In addition to her teaching and research,

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.620
Professor Gage writes for
numerous journals and magazines,

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.510
including "The New
Yorker," "New York Times,"

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.130
and "Washington Post".

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.970
In 2009, Professor Gage received
the Sarai Ribicoff Award

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.250
for Teaching Excellence in Yale College.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:06.960
In 2015, she was--

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.330
She has a very long list of honors.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.070
I'm gonna give you like a short pressie

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.500
because I don't want take
up, you know, airtime.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.440
So in 2015, she was elected
to serve as the first chair

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.960
of Yale's Faculty of
Arts and Sciences Senate.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.170
From 2017 to 2021, she
served as the director

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.380
of Yale's Brady Johnson
program in Grand Strategy,

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.320
and in 2021, president
Joe Biden nominated her

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.660
to serve on the National
Humanities Council,

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:31.950
which is the advisory board

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.200
for the National Endowment
for the Humanities.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.750
At Yale with Professor Elizabeth Hinton,

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.910
she leads the workshop
in modern U.S. history,

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.550
a monthly speaker series for
faculty and graduate students

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:42.960
to discuss the latest scholarship

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.880
in 20th century U.S. history,

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.280
and she's also deeply one of our own.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.080
She's a graduate of Yale
University and from the American

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.410
and has a degree in American
studies and finished her PhD

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:56.820
at Columbia in history.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.470
So without further ado,

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.810
I would like to turn the
floor over to Professor Gage.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:03.060
Thank you so much,
Beverly, for coming today.

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:06.883
(applause)

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.290
- Well, thanks for that
generous introduction.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:14.040
Thanks to the Franke's for
their support of this program

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:18.570
and to all of you for turning
out on a nice spring day

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:23.130
in a windowless room at
the end of the semester.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:27.480
So I think the plan for today
is that I'm gonna talk for,

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:30.030
you know, about 40, 45 minutes and really,

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:33.150
just try to give you an
overview of this book

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:38.150
that I published in November
that is, as Priya suggested,

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:41.040
sort of making the rounds in the world,

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.720
and then leave time for
some real Q&amp;A at the end.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:50.370
I've found that in talking
about Jay Edgar Hoover,

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.460
who, as you will hear,
lived a very long time

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.110
and did a very many things
that my overview is useful,

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.480
but that many people come to this subject

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:04.110
from many different directions
and many different interests,

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.600
and have many different
points of intersection.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.750
So I wanna make sure
that we get to the things

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:12.180
that interest you about Hoover,

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.253
as well as the things that interest me.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:20.130
When most Americans think about
Hoover in the 21st century,

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:23.130
one of two images comes to mind.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.910
The first, and I think
most prominent is of Hoover

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:30.780
as sort of the great
villain of the 20th century,

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.800
someone who abused
American's civil liberties,

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.800
was one of the most
manipulative, powerful,

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.683
and most hated figures
in all of Washington.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:49.590
The second and slightly
more prurient set of images

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:52.410
that often come to mind,
they're really more a product

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.600
of the 1990s, are a set of questions

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.810
about Hoover's sexuality,
his private life,

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:02.100
most prominently the story
that emerged in the 1990s

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:05.400
that Hoover liked to
wear women's clothing.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.840
So I'm gonna get to both aspects
of that part of his image

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:13.860
as we go through the talk and
as I did in the book itself.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:18.480
But to start, I wanted to
present a different image.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:22.650
This is Hoover's funeral in May of 1972.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:28.230
He was the first civil servant ever

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:32.130
to lie in state at the U.S. Capitol,

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.040
and there are a couple of things
that I think are important

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.070
about starting with this image.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:41.700
First, is that by the time he died,

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:46.700
he had been in office as
director of the FBI for 48 years.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.730
So almost half a century.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.340
This is a cartoon that
I reprint in the book,

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:56.250
and I highly recommend
picking up a copy of the book,

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:59.940
even if you don't buy it,
because I managed to persuade

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:04.320
my publisher to publish 130 photos,

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.810
which was a little bit of a struggle,

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:08.700
but I'm gonna show some
of them here today.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.590
But there are a lot of good ones in there.

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.380
This is one of them, and this
is a cartoon that was done

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:18.380
in 1968, when Richard Nixon
was elected president,

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.570
that shows all of the different presidents

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.130
that Hoover had served under,

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:27.120
from Calvin Coolidge to Richard Nixon.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.660
Hoover became head of
this little organization

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:35.100
known as the Bureau of
Investigation in 1924,

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.190
under Calvin Coolidge,
as you can see there.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:42.510
He stayed on through the
presidency of Herbert Hoover.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:47.160
So that was into the dawn
of the Great Depression,

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:52.140
the crises of the late
1920s and early 1930s.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:56.070
He then remained for all three plus terms

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.860
of Franklin Roosevelt's presidency.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.270
This is Roosevelt seated.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:03.060
Hoover is the kind of youngish man

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:05.100
standing right behind him.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:09.330
That's Roosevelt signing
some crime legislation.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.820
So he stayed on through the New Deal,

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:13.920
the lead up to the Second World War,

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:16.800
and all of the Second World War.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:19.920
He was then there through both terms

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.870
of Harry Truman's presidency.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:23.970
So that's the dawn of the Cold War,

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.430
the years of McCarthyism.

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.440
He then stayed on through both terms

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.840
of Dwight Eisenhower's presidency,

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.440
so the tail end of
McCarthyism, the conservative,

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:38.310
as well as liberal politics of the 1950s.

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:42.300
He then stayed on through
John Kennedy's presidency.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:45.000
He was there under Lyndon Johnson,

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:50.000
through many of the dramatic
changes in crises of the 1960s.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:54.630
And then he remained under Richard Nixon

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:58.830
and died toward the end
of Nixon's first term,

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:03.723
still with the same job,
the head of the FBI.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.800
So when I have told people
over the last decade plus

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:09.450
that it took me to write this book,

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:12.840
that I'm writing a biography
of Jay Edgar Hoover,

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.610
often their first question is,

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:17.490
why in the world would
you want to do that?

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:20.040
And I think often what
people mean by that question

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:23.670
is why would you want
to spend so much time

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:26.640
with this villain, with this terrible man,

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:29.970
who is reputed to have and in fact,

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:33.210
did so many terrible things?

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:37.530
And one piece of that answer
is simply this sweep of time

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:40.770
that Hoover was there for so long,

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:42.990
doing so many different things,

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:46.410
that he's not only
interesting in his own right

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:51.000
as an individual, but is
interesting to me as a vehicle

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:55.920
into some larger story about
20th century American politics,

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:59.430
and I think tells us some
things that aren't as evident

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:02.250
when we look in other places.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:05.070
So I just wanna mention
a few of those things

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:07.500
and a few of those big themes

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:10.080
that I tried to emphasize in this book,

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:13.200
and then I'm gonna run
pretty efficiently I think,

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:15.540
through the book as a whole,

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:18.063
and then open this up for Q&amp;A.

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:22.020
So one thing that I think that we can see,

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.560
when we look at Hoover as a vehicle

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.750
for thinking about the 20th century,

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:32.190
rather than some other ways of
thinking about our politics,

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:35.100
is a story that's as much about government

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:37.440
as it is about politics.

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:42.060
My book is called "G-Man,"
which stands for government man,

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:44.250
and it's called that because
that was the nickname

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:48.840
for FBI agents beginning in the 1930s.

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:52.320
So it's a catchy title in that sense,

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.780
but it also conveys
one of these big themes

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:56.100
that I try to get across,

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:59.580
which is that this is a
story that is about politics,

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:01.950
but it's about governance
and in particular,

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:04.200
it's about the growth of the state,

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:08.580
the federal government over
the course of the 20th century.

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:12.720
When Hoover became director
of the FBI in 1924,

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:15.840
it was this little organization

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:18.510
known as the Bureau of Investigation.

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:20.340
There were a few hundred agents,

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:23.550
they didn't actually
have a whole lot to do.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:27.360
By the time he died in 1972,

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:31.290
he had about 16,000 employees.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:35.370
The FBI's jurisdiction
had expanded dramatically,

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:38.190
and the size of the federal
government as a whole,

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:40.950
had expanded really dramatically.

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:44.463
I think when we look at our
political history, very often,

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:48.210
certainly in public
discourse to some degree,

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:50.370
in the historical profession as well,

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:53.790
there tends to be a lot of
emphasis on political parties,

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:56.730
political figures, elections, ideology,

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:59.010
and Hoover's part of that story,

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:01.050
but he sort of turns our lens

00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:05.910
toward the administrative
state, toward this vast world

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:09.030
of executive power and
administrative power

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:13.680
that really operates outside
of electoral control,

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:17.640
in many ways, and that in
some ways becomes, I think,

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:19.950
the bigger story of the 20th century,

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:22.020
or at least one of the most significant

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:24.510
political stories there,

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:29.070
and within that, Hoover is a
really interesting character.

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:32.100
He is someone who was
really interesting to me,

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:36.180
as an ideological figure,
because I think he represents

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:38.970
two strains of our political story

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:43.140
that we don't often see
combined very well together.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:46.620
One is that he was,
from his earliest years,

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:51.270
really a true believer in
this vision of federal power,

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:54.600
in what we might describe
as a kind of progressive

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:57.780
or liberal vision of state power

00:12:57.780 --> 00:12:59.670
that involved professionalism,

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:02.730
that involved nonpartisan service,

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:05.640
that involved career
service to the government,

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:08.910
that involved the use
of science and expertise

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:11.550
to serve the public good.

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:14.700
And on the other hand, he was
throughout this whole time,

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:19.700
a really deep ideological
conservative on questions of race,

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:24.000
on questions of religion, on
questions of law and order,

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.980
and above all, on anti-communism,

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:29.700
which is really the big
theme of Hoover's life.

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:34.700
And I think, you know, what
fascinated me about his story

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.960
and what we don't see very often,

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:39.180
I think in our own politics today,

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:43.890
is the combining of these
two traditions into a career

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:47.190
that lasted for a very, very long time.

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:49.380
In the book, I characterized
him as something

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:51.810
called a conservative state builder,

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:54.390
but someone who essentially used the tools

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:57.330
of the liberal state,
including the New Deal,

00:13:57.330 --> 00:14:01.170
which is really the moment
that the FBI gets its power,

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:04.680
but used those tools
really to serve a variety

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:07.860
of conservative causes in the end

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.130
and made those two pieces work together.

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:14.190
So that's sort of the
political puzzle of the book.

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:16.410
And then of course,
one of the other things

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:20.730
that drew me to Hoover
was simply the opportunity

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:24.770
to reinterpret a figure that
actually hadn't been revisited

00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:27.060
in a very long time.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.940
There were about 30 years have passed

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:33.420
since the last round
of Hoover biographies.

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:36.960
And even when I started working
on this book, which was,

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:41.960
I will admit, in 2009,
so about 13 years ago,

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:44.700
even then, it was clear not only

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:46.530
that Hoover was in many ways,

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:50.310
ripe for reinterpretation, but
that there were a whole host

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:53.790
of new files that had
come out about Hoover

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:57.750
and it seemed like an
opportunity to delve into those,

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:01.080
in particular, files that
had come out since the end

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:04.410
of the Cold War, that
I think really help us

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:08.100
to rethink some of the ways
that we narrate the struggles

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:11.850
over communism and anti-communism
in the 20th century.

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:14.520
But lots of other files
that have come out as well,

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:17.640
some of them through the
Freedom of Information Act,

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.320
some of them files that I myself,

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:21.840
acquired through the
Freedom of Information Act,

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:26.840
others that were released
in a routine way by the FBI.

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:29.640
So I am, you know, in my core,

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:32.400
really a kind of archive
nerd, a history nerd.

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:36.390
I love sitting there with
these giant piles of files,

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:39.930
trying to figure out things
that we didn't know before.

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:44.930
And I will say, the amount
of information available

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:48.390
about a figure like Hoover
is both part of the appeal

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:50.910
of writing this book, which
I will warn those of you

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:54.183
who haven't seen it, is
about 800 pages long.

00:15:55.048 --> 00:15:58.110
And of course, is one of the
big challenges of doing it.

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:02.070
How do you not read absolutely everything,

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:04.410
but read enough to find out things

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:06.840
that are really new and interesting?

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:10.560
And of course, in Hoover's
case, that basic problem

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:14.160
of expansive but limited research,

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:17.670
is complicated by the
fact that he ran a very,

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:22.670
very secretive bureaucracy,
and so many of those files are,

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:27.360
even today, 51 years after his death,

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:30.960
are still redacted, are hard to access,

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:32.820
are hard to figure out.

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:37.080
So I'd be happy to talk more
about that research project

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:40.890
as it evolved over the
period that I worked on it

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:43.410
when we get to the Q&amp;A.

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:46.470
But all told, those variety of factors,

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:48.510
the political puzzle that's available,

00:16:48.510 --> 00:16:52.350
Hoover as a vehicle into
so many different things

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:54.240
over the course of the 20th century,

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:57.840
and then the wealth of new
material that was available,

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:01.080
made me think that this was a good moment

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:04.950
to revisit this figure
that I think has been,

00:17:04.950 --> 00:17:08.190
and still is, a household name,

00:17:08.190 --> 00:17:10.530
but who has been a pretty
one-dimensional figure

00:17:10.530 --> 00:17:11.430
in lots of ways.

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:15.090
And my goal in writing a
biography of him was not only

00:17:15.090 --> 00:17:19.020
to intervene in this set
of academic conversations,

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:22.140
but to really sort of take
him from where he's been,

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:24.360
which is this one-dimensional figure,

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:27.930
sort of seen as this rogue
actor in American history,

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:32.930
and really bring him back
into a more complicated story

00:17:33.330 --> 00:17:36.510
about the 20th century
and really bring him back

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:39.300
into the center of
things and to the center

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:42.750
of a political story
about this period of time.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:46.170
Because while we tend to
think of him as someone

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:49.590
that everyone loves to hate,
there are very few people

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:51.900
in our own day and age

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:55.140
who want to claim a figure like Hoover.

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:59.220
Nonetheless, during his lifetime,
he was enormously popular,

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:02.700
he was enormously
influential, and of course,

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:05.190
any one of those presidents,
all eight of them,

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:06.900
four Democrats, four Republicans,

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:10.350
could have fired him at
any moment and did not.

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:14.520
And so the story of his
longevity, his popularity,

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:18.930
the sources of his support,
is also a piece of his story

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:20.820
that I wanted to tell in this book,

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:21.870
and I think in many ways,

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:26.310
maybe the most counterintuitive
piece of that story,

00:18:26.310 --> 00:18:31.310
given how widely hated and
condemned he is in our own day.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:34.440
So I wanted to take a few minutes now

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:39.440
and just put a little more
detail onto this story of Hoover,

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:44.220
run relatively quickly through
the sections of the book

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:47.340
so you can get a sense of
the kind of amazing scope

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:50.580
of his life, the variety
of things that I touch upon

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:52.800
in the book, and then,
as I said, hopefully,

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:56.043
we can just open this up for conversation.

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:02.940
So this is a real, you know,
birth to death biography.

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:04.980
I found that in writing a biography,

00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:08.640
one of the advantages
of the biographical form

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:11.280
is that it does give you a place to start

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:14.040
and a place to end, and
that's what I have done.

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:17.430
I did not try to upend
the biographical form.

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:19.410
And these are some of
the earliest pictures

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:20.243
we have of Hoover.

00:19:20.243 --> 00:19:22.530
I should note this is
the only actual photo

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:23.850
of Hoover doing what?

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:27.000
Wearing a dress, his baby photo.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:31.140
But Hoover is a child of Washington, D.C..

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:36.140
He was born in Washington D.C.
in 1895, and he never left.

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:39.660
He's born there in 1895.

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:42.930
He dies there in 1972.

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:47.930
And his biography really is a
story about Washington D.C.,

00:19:48.870 --> 00:19:51.390
what that city meant and how it changed

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:54.210
over the course of his lifetime.

00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:57.000
And during his early years,
I think there are two themes

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:01.350
that are really important
for his future development.

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:04.410
The first is that he was born

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:07.320
into a world of government service.

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:09.540
He was born on Capitol Hill,

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:12.720
just a few blocks behind the U.S. Capitol,

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:15.330
and he was born into
a family that already,

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:20.160
in the late 19th century,
had decades of service

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:24.390
to the federal government in
the sort of professionalized

00:20:24.390 --> 00:20:28.020
and of the executive
branch in the few places

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:30.810
that really were part of
the federal Civil Service

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:32.100
at that point, right?

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:34.980
The government in the late 19th century,

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:37.680
particularly the
executive branch, is much,

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:40.140
much smaller and has many fewer duties

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:42.810
than what we are accustomed to today.

00:20:42.810 --> 00:20:45.900
But nonetheless, Hoover's
family had been involved

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:47.850
in the government and it was the world

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:49.170
that he was born into.

00:20:49.170 --> 00:20:53.040
His father was sort of a
mid-level civil servant,

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:56.280
and it was what Hoover saw all around him.

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:59.670
Washington D.C. as the national capital

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:02.370
and as the federal city
during those years,

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:04.980
also meant some very particular things,

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:08.430
the most notable of which is
that Washington D.C. residents

00:21:08.430 --> 00:21:09.780
could not vote.

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:11.610
They did not join political parties,

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:13.710
and they could not vote,
either for their own

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:17.220
municipal government or
in federal elections.

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:20.430
So Hoover was not only
born into this tradition

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:24.120
of civil service, but
he was born into a world

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:28.620
that stood sort of proudly
apart from partisanship

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:31.650
that was a world of career service,

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:34.680
and in which he had very little access

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:37.740
to any sort of democratic participation.

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:39.390
So he never saw his parents vote,

00:21:39.390 --> 00:21:41.940
he himself never joined a political party,

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:44.733
and he never voted in a single election.

00:21:45.570 --> 00:21:48.060
So that's one piece of
the Washington tradition

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:51.420
that I think is really
critical for understanding

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:54.330
where he came from and who he became.

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:57.000
The other piece that's really
critical is that he was born

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:00.810
into Washington that was
essentially a conservative,

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:03.390
southern city in the late 19th century.

00:22:03.390 --> 00:22:06.360
It was a city that was actively undergoing

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:09.270
a process of racial segregation,

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:11.610
had been a pretty multiracial city

00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:16.290
in the mid and late 19th century,
but during his childhood,

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:19.860
was actively building racial barriers,

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:22.290
and he participated in and witnessed

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:24.870
and was shaped by that process.

00:22:24.870 --> 00:22:28.980
He went to segregated public
schools in Washington D.C.

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:33.960
and then he went on to a
segregated private university,

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:36.690
George Washington University.

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:39.060
At the moment that he
joined federal employment

00:22:39.060 --> 00:22:42.690
under the Wilson administration
was precisely the moment

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:45.360
that the federal government
was self-consciously

00:22:45.360 --> 00:22:48.720
and actively segregating
federal employment

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:50.910
throughout the executive branch.

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:54.180
So these two pieces, this
government service tradition,

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:57.300
a tradition of racial conservatism,

00:22:57.300 --> 00:23:00.900
of active segregationist processes go on

00:23:00.900 --> 00:23:05.900
to be really important to
Hoover as he develops over time.

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:10.470
As I said, he went to the
Washington D.C. public schools.

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:14.160
He does not come from a wealthy family.

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:16.020
So he goes through the
public school system.

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:20.130
This is him here as a high school cadet.

00:23:20.130 --> 00:23:23.460
He was a kind of star student.

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:27.810
We, I think, tend to think
of him as a rather plodding

00:23:27.810 --> 00:23:30.630
and you know, fixed old man,

00:23:30.630 --> 00:23:34.770
but he was apparently quite
the energetic, vibrant,

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:37.320
and ambitious young
student in high school.

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:39.600
He was the valedictorian of his class.

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:42.870
He was the cadet leader
of his high school class,

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:47.400
and he was also a star
debater in high school.

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:51.510
He, from there, went on to
a really critical period

00:23:51.510 --> 00:23:54.960
of his life, which is
his years as a student

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:59.160
at George Washington
University that matter for,

00:23:59.160 --> 00:24:02.040
I would say, three key reasons.

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:06.120
Number one is that in
order to earn his way

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:08.520
through law school at GW,

00:24:08.520 --> 00:24:11.370
which was an undergraduate degree then,

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:13.260
he had to have a day job.

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:15.990
GW at that point, was
basically a night school

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:18.720
for future federal civil servants,

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:21.810
and most of them had
government jobs by day

00:24:21.810 --> 00:24:24.060
and then went to GW at night.

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:26.640
Hoover was one of those students.

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:30.570
He got a job at the Library of Congress,

00:24:30.570 --> 00:24:33.270
helping to sort through and establish

00:24:33.270 --> 00:24:37.080
the Library of Congress
classification system,

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:41.880
and he learned very early
on there to build files,

00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:43.290
to learn to classify things,

00:24:43.290 --> 00:24:45.360
a whole set of kind of administrative

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:49.410
and information technology
tools that prove important.

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:51.630
The second is GW is his introduction

00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:53.760
to the study of the law.

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:57.570
And then the third is
what you see up here,

00:24:57.570 --> 00:24:59.250
which is that at GW,

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:03.180
he joined a fraternity called Kappa Alpha.

00:25:03.180 --> 00:25:06.000
Kappa Alpha became not
only important to him

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:10.860
as a young man at GW, but
became the source of much

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:15.600
of his hiring at the FBI
in the years to follow.

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:19.620
Now, Kappa Alpha is a really
fascinating institution.

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:21.780
Something that I didn't know much about,

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:24.840
hadn't been written about
much before this biography.

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:27.510
But it turns out the Kappa Alpha

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:31.170
was a explicitly southern fraternity,

00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:35.430
had been founded in 1865 in
the wake of the Civil War,

00:25:35.430 --> 00:25:39.900
essentially to carry on the
lost cause of the white South.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:44.900
Revered Robert E. Lee had very
explicit, sort of racialized,

00:25:45.540 --> 00:25:48.570
lost cause, pro-confederate politics,

00:25:48.570 --> 00:25:52.470
and by the time Hoover joined
in the early 20th century,

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:55.170
was a real meeting place of power

00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:57.600
for segregationist southern Democrats

00:25:57.600 --> 00:26:00.540
serving in Washington D.C..

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:03.720
So Kappa Alpha becomes a
place where you can see

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:07.830
that Hoover's racial
ideology is hardening,

00:26:07.830 --> 00:26:11.640
really becoming much more
specific, and it's also a place

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:14.280
where he begins to develop
a political network

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:17.430
that becomes incredibly important to him,

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:19.620
a world of southern Democrats

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:24.060
and a world of access to political power.

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:28.440
These images are a couple
of examples of Kappa Alpha,

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:32.550
which he remained involved
in for all of his life.

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:33.870
The first one up there,

00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:36.930
which is a novel called "The Klansman,"

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:41.250
which became the basis for
the film, "Birth of a Nation,"

00:26:41.250 --> 00:26:43.290
written by a guy named Thomas Dixon,

00:26:43.290 --> 00:26:48.290
who is one of the most famous
Kappa Alphas of Hoover's era.

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:50.100
He was still alive,

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:53.610
was this sort of famous
segregationist voice, and of course,

00:26:53.610 --> 00:26:56.100
both the "Klansmen" and
the "Birth of a Nation"

00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:59.467
are glorifications of the Ku Klux Klan.

00:26:59.467 --> 00:27:01.410
"Birth of a Nation" came out while Hoover

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:02.940
was a college student.

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:06.390
His Kappa Alpha fraternity
gets together, they screen it,

00:27:06.390 --> 00:27:09.450
they're very tightly tied into the world

00:27:09.450 --> 00:27:13.350
of the Wilson Administration,
where that kind of politics

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:17.070
is holding sway, and this
continues for much of his life.

00:27:17.070 --> 00:27:21.120
As I said, he retains a kind
of lifelong allegiance to

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:23.130
and alliance with Kappa Alpha,

00:27:23.130 --> 00:27:26.430
draws a lot of the
first-generation of FBI officials,

00:27:26.430 --> 00:27:28.920
both out of GW and Kappa Alpha.

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:32.100
But as you can see, this
is an image from the 1950s,

00:27:32.100 --> 00:27:35.070
when Kappa Alpha is really
critical in popularizing

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:39.060
the confederate flag as
a symbol of resistance

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:40.320
to the civil rights movement.

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:42.120
And then over there,

00:27:42.120 --> 00:27:45.540
a kind of lifetime achievement
certificate for Hoover

00:27:45.540 --> 00:27:48.060
in the early '60s from Kappa Alpha.

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:52.410
I should note that they are
still around, this fraternity.

00:27:52.410 --> 00:27:56.550
Many of these issues are still
live issues for Kappa Alpha.

00:27:56.550 --> 00:28:01.200
So I recommend Googling Kappa
Alpha in our own present day.

00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:02.910
I should also say they're not thrilled

00:28:02.910 --> 00:28:06.300
with my depiction of them in this book.

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:08.320
I have heard from some Kappa Alphas

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:11.790
on those sorts of questions.

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:15.450
So that is his really
important, I think, you know,

00:28:15.450 --> 00:28:20.190
formative period in college 1913 to 1917.

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:24.090
And then something happens
that's really important

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:27.630
and is one of these
kind of flukes of timing

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:29.850
that matters not only for Hoover,

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:32.040
but really for for the nation,

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:34.620
for the 20th century,
for the United States,

00:28:34.620 --> 00:28:39.300
which is that he happens to
graduate from college in 1917,

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:41.910
at exactly the moment
that the United States

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:44.700
is entering the First World War.

00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:47.220
He does not, though he had been, you know,

00:28:47.220 --> 00:28:50.910
a cadet captain, does not go
on to serve in the military.

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:54.630
Instead he moves straight
into the Justice Department.

00:28:54.630 --> 00:28:58.500
It is his first job out of
college, and he never leaves.

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:01.980
So he spends 48 years as head of the FBI.

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:06.240
Ultimately, he spends a total
of 55 years as an employee

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:11.220
of the Justice Department,
beginning in 1917.

00:29:11.220 --> 00:29:13.350
He moves into the Justice Department

00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:17.820
at another really critical
period in American history,

00:29:17.820 --> 00:29:20.940
a period in which the
Justice Department's duties

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:24.180
are expanding very
dramatically with the war,

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:26.010
and in which they have a deep need

00:29:26.010 --> 00:29:29.940
for sort of energetic young
lawyers to help them figure out

00:29:29.940 --> 00:29:32.400
how to manage those new duties,

00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:35.040
things like draft enforcement as the U.S.

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:40.040
is engaged in large
scale selective service

00:29:40.350 --> 00:29:42.930
and draft for the first time.

00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:46.260
In many ways, there are a
whole new set of speech laws

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:47.790
that come in during the war,

00:29:47.790 --> 00:29:50.640
the Espionage Act and the Sedition Act,

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:54.270
aimed at war dissenters,
left-wingers, revolutionaries,

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:57.480
the Justice Department has to
figure out what to do there.

00:29:57.480 --> 00:30:01.410
And they are also given the
task of figuring out a system

00:30:01.410 --> 00:30:04.200
of internment for German nationals

00:30:04.200 --> 00:30:07.350
living in the United
States and that is actually

00:30:07.350 --> 00:30:10.860
Hoover's first set of duties
at the Justice Department,

00:30:10.860 --> 00:30:15.060
German internment and German
registration during the war.

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:18.660
Again, something that goes
on to matter an awful lot.

00:30:18.660 --> 00:30:21.510
From the moment that he
enters the federal government,

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:25.800
he is charged with these new
experiments in figuring out,

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:29.010
you know, who's dangerous,
who's not dangerous,

00:30:29.010 --> 00:30:31.200
who belongs, who doesn't belong,

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:32.910
whose opinion is acceptable,

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:34.920
whose opinion is not acceptable,

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:38.250
and how are you going
to use this new legal

00:30:38.250 --> 00:30:41.340
and administrative state
that is in the works

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:43.590
to police those lines.

00:30:43.590 --> 00:30:47.700
These are some early archival
examples of his judgments

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:52.700
as a young lawyer about
various German cases.

00:30:52.950 --> 00:30:57.150
Turns out that he is so good
at that, that at the age of 24,

00:30:57.150 --> 00:31:01.170
as the war comes to an
end and these questions

00:31:01.170 --> 00:31:03.840
of what all of this new apparatus

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:06.150
is going to look like and do with the war,

00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:09.540
he gets the first big
promotion of his life,

00:31:09.540 --> 00:31:10.770
the thing that really brings him

00:31:10.770 --> 00:31:13.020
into the Bureau of Investigation,

00:31:13.020 --> 00:31:15.720
and that is a promotion
to the head of something

00:31:15.720 --> 00:31:17.250
called the Radical Division

00:31:17.250 --> 00:31:18.960
within the Justice Department

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:21.390
and the Bureau of Investigation.

00:31:21.390 --> 00:31:24.720
It's really the federal
government's first peacetime

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:28.110
experiment in how to conduct surveillance

00:31:28.110 --> 00:31:31.230
of left-wing radicals
in the United States,

00:31:31.230 --> 00:31:34.740
particularly anarchists and communists.

00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:36.810
World War I had also, of course,

00:31:36.810 --> 00:31:39.300
produced the Bolshevik Revolution,

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:42.390
had produced a whole new set of concerns

00:31:42.390 --> 00:31:45.750
around the globe about
revolutionary sentiment,

00:31:45.750 --> 00:31:49.350
the rise of communism,
and in the United States

00:31:49.350 --> 00:31:52.830
helps to produce a a
pretty dramatic red scare

00:31:52.830 --> 00:31:55.620
beginning in 1919.

00:31:55.620 --> 00:31:58.170
Hoover is in the thick of that.

00:31:58.170 --> 00:32:02.070
He is as a very, very young man,

00:32:02.070 --> 00:32:04.920
one of the architects of the Palmer Raids,

00:32:04.920 --> 00:32:09.420
which are a series of deportation
raids aimed at anarchists

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:14.160
and communists that take
place in late 1919 and 1920.

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:16.920
He spent a lot of the
rest of his career denying

00:32:16.920 --> 00:32:19.260
that he had ever been
involved in the Palmer Raids

00:32:19.260 --> 00:32:22.050
because they became very controversial

00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:26.070
and sort of notorious symbols
of civil liberties abuses.

00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:28.020
But it's clear from the archival record

00:32:28.020 --> 00:32:31.230
that he was deeply involved,
that he helped to plan

00:32:31.230 --> 00:32:35.370
those raids and that he learned
quite a lot very quickly

00:32:35.370 --> 00:32:37.590
during those early years.

00:32:37.590 --> 00:32:41.880
One is really an
introduction to the language

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:45.060
and set of ideas that become
the cause of his life,

00:32:45.060 --> 00:32:47.490
which is the struggle against communism.

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:52.490
He's initiated into that
very early on as a young man.

00:32:52.890 --> 00:32:55.590
He writes some of the
first official briefs

00:32:55.590 --> 00:32:58.320
for the federal government
about the nature

00:32:58.320 --> 00:33:02.520
of the new communist parties
forming in the United States.

00:33:02.520 --> 00:33:05.850
He really stakes a lot
of his career in being

00:33:05.850 --> 00:33:10.110
one of the great government
experts on communism,

00:33:10.110 --> 00:33:14.100
and he's saying very
similar things in the 1940s,

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:17.130
he's saying very similar
things in the 1970s,

00:33:17.130 --> 00:33:21.963
to what he first starts to
articulate in '19 and 1920,

00:33:23.790 --> 00:33:25.740
during these very early years.

00:33:25.740 --> 00:33:28.020
And then the second
piece is it's the moment

00:33:28.020 --> 00:33:32.010
that he first comes into
contact and then conflict

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:34.680
with new civil liberties organizations

00:33:34.680 --> 00:33:36.270
that are just getting their start

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:39.210
and that he's going to
kind of be in a dance with

00:33:39.210 --> 00:33:41.430
for the rest of his career.

00:33:41.430 --> 00:33:43.680
Most notably the ACLU,

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:46.110
which has its origins
in the First World War,

00:33:46.110 --> 00:33:49.290
these struggles during the Red Scare.

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:54.240
From there, despite the
controversies of the Palmer Raids,

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:59.240
Hoover does get another
quick promotion in 1921.

00:33:59.670 --> 00:34:02.850
As the democrats leave power,
the republicans come in,

00:34:02.850 --> 00:34:06.420
Hoover manages to stay
on and gets a promotion

00:34:06.420 --> 00:34:10.560
to become assistant director
of the Bureau of Investigation

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:15.560
at the age of 26, and then
in 1924, three years later,

00:34:15.810 --> 00:34:18.900
at the rip old age of 29,

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:22.140
becomes the director of the
Bureau of Investigation,

00:34:22.140 --> 00:34:26.040
which is the job that he holds
for the rest of his life.

00:34:26.040 --> 00:34:29.910
So I've spent a long time
on Hoover's early years,

00:34:29.910 --> 00:34:31.860
and I'm gonna move a little more quickly

00:34:31.860 --> 00:34:34.170
through the next sections of the book

00:34:34.170 --> 00:34:36.210
so that we can get to the Q&amp;A,

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:39.330
but I think you can see
during these early years,

00:34:39.330 --> 00:34:41.400
a lot of the themes developing

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:45.270
that he is going to then
learn to build upon,

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:49.050
to operationalize over
the course of his career

00:34:49.050 --> 00:34:50.610
at the head of the Bureau.

00:34:50.610 --> 00:34:55.610
And I think one of the puzzles
and interesting aspects

00:34:55.650 --> 00:34:59.490
of a figure like Hoover
is that on the one hand,

00:34:59.490 --> 00:35:04.230
he has a very fixed set
of ideas about government,

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:08.340
about race, about
communism, about religion,

00:35:08.340 --> 00:35:11.970
that are there from the
moment he begins this work

00:35:11.970 --> 00:35:13.770
to the moment that he dies,

00:35:13.770 --> 00:35:16.110
they're incredibly consistent, right?

00:35:16.110 --> 00:35:19.440
His words sound incredibly similar

00:35:19.440 --> 00:35:21.900
over the course of that period.

00:35:21.900 --> 00:35:23.130
And at the same time,

00:35:23.130 --> 00:35:27.330
he is someone who is incredibly
adaptable, able to learn,

00:35:27.330 --> 00:35:32.160
and in particular, able to
respond to and build upon crises.

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:35.100
And in many ways, the
story of the 20th century

00:35:35.100 --> 00:35:40.100
is a story of various crises
that then produce big shifts

00:35:41.010 --> 00:35:45.030
in the government, growth
of the security state,

00:35:45.030 --> 00:35:47.430
as well as the social welfare state,

00:35:47.430 --> 00:35:51.840
and Hoover is incredibly skilled
at adapting to those crises

00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:56.070
and delivering on what he
thinks the people above him,

00:35:56.070 --> 00:36:00.240
particularly in the White House,
as well as those below him,

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:03.720
his popular constituency want from him,

00:36:03.720 --> 00:36:07.230
from the FBI and from the
federal government as a whole,

00:36:07.230 --> 00:36:09.063
in those moments of crisis.

00:36:10.680 --> 00:36:12.750
So the first part of
the book really focuses

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:17.610
on his early years from 1895 to 1924,

00:36:17.610 --> 00:36:19.200
kind of getting him to the moment

00:36:19.200 --> 00:36:21.750
of becoming head of the Bureau.

00:36:21.750 --> 00:36:26.750
Part two then moves into
looking at the 1920s and 1930s

00:36:28.080 --> 00:36:32.310
as the periods when he
really built the institution

00:36:32.310 --> 00:36:35.253
that we know as the FBI.

00:36:36.323 --> 00:36:38.550
It is very strange for us to think about,

00:36:38.550 --> 00:36:41.670
but when Hoover came in in 1924

00:36:41.670 --> 00:36:44.610
as this young new director of the bureau,

00:36:44.610 --> 00:36:47.970
he really is brought in as a reformer.

00:36:47.970 --> 00:36:51.330
He's brought in as a kind
of energetic young reformer,

00:36:51.330 --> 00:36:55.230
trained in the law, trained
in information technology,

00:36:55.230 --> 00:36:58.680
trained in a certain
kind of professionalism,

00:36:58.680 --> 00:37:02.460
and his job is to professionalize
and clean up the bureau,

00:37:02.460 --> 00:37:06.300
which has been A, kind of tiny backwater,

00:37:06.300 --> 00:37:10.980
but B, a mess even at
that, full of corruption,

00:37:10.980 --> 00:37:13.680
notorious for its abuses
of civil liberties.

00:37:13.680 --> 00:37:15.180
And so when Hoover comes in,

00:37:15.180 --> 00:37:18.660
the word is there's a new man in town.

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:23.660
He is a young professional,
steeped in progressive ideas

00:37:24.330 --> 00:37:28.710
about science, about
expertise, about education,

00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:32.340
and really, his vision when
he becomes head of the Bureau

00:37:32.340 --> 00:37:37.260
is to remake it as a kind of
elite law enforcement agency

00:37:37.260 --> 00:37:41.520
that is going to be a model
of these progressive era ideas

00:37:41.520 --> 00:37:45.180
about facts and science and expertise

00:37:45.180 --> 00:37:48.990
and education within the
world of law enforcement.

00:37:48.990 --> 00:37:51.870
As you can see here, merit,

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:56.310
often he's contrasted with
figures like Sherlock Holmes,

00:37:56.310 --> 00:37:58.230
the kind of rogue detective, right?

00:37:58.230 --> 00:38:01.890
And Hoover's men are going
to be a different kind

00:38:01.890 --> 00:38:03.243
of professional agent.

00:38:04.200 --> 00:38:06.510
He promises not to do lots of things,

00:38:06.510 --> 00:38:10.080
says they're unsuited to the
work of something like the FBI.

00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:12.000
They're never gonna open people's mail,

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:13.830
they're never gonna wire tap,

00:38:13.830 --> 00:38:17.670
they're never going to coerce
anyone into doing anything.

00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:20.760
Of course, hmm, this changes
a little bit over time,

00:38:20.760 --> 00:38:21.753
as you will see.

00:38:22.704 --> 00:38:25.800
And he sets about not only kind of,

00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:28.440
as he would describe it,
cleansing the bureaucracy,

00:38:28.440 --> 00:38:30.450
but bringing in a new generation

00:38:30.450 --> 00:38:33.300
of college-educated
lawyers and accountants,

00:38:33.300 --> 00:38:35.760
who are supposed to be
these kind of shining,

00:38:35.760 --> 00:38:39.240
white collar professional
law enforcement symbols.

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:43.590
Many of them come straight out
of his beloved institutions,

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:47.490
both George Washington
University and Kappa Alpha.

00:38:47.490 --> 00:38:51.630
This is a newspaper feature
on the many young GW men

00:38:51.630 --> 00:38:56.040
who are remaking the bureau
in the '20s and '30s.

00:38:56.040 --> 00:38:59.430
The most important of those
men is this fellow here,

00:38:59.430 --> 00:39:01.560
named Clyde Tolson.

00:39:01.560 --> 00:39:05.700
Clyde Tolson was a graduate of GW.

00:39:05.700 --> 00:39:10.110
He graduated a few years after
Hoover entered the Bureau

00:39:10.110 --> 00:39:14.580
in 1928, and went on very quickly to rise

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:18.150
to the number two position at the Bureau

00:39:18.150 --> 00:39:22.530
and to occupy that position
at Hoover's right hand

00:39:22.530 --> 00:39:25.503
for the rest of his life as well.

00:39:26.340 --> 00:39:28.380
Now, Clyde Tolson, of course,

00:39:28.380 --> 00:39:30.120
if you know anything
about J. Edgar Hoover,

00:39:30.120 --> 00:39:33.690
is not just the associate
director of the FBI,

00:39:33.690 --> 00:39:37.650
but is it also in many
ways Hoover's life partner,

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:39.570
his social partner,

00:39:39.570 --> 00:39:44.520
and the closest thing that
Hoover ever had to a spouse.

00:39:44.520 --> 00:39:46.290
There are lots of, I think,

00:39:46.290 --> 00:39:50.280
interesting things to say
about Hoover's sexuality,

00:39:50.280 --> 00:39:52.500
and I'm happy to talk more about that.

00:39:52.500 --> 00:39:55.410
I do write quite a lot
about it in the book,

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:57.963
but I'll just say a few things right now.

00:39:58.830 --> 00:40:02.280
One is that Tolson is by far

00:40:02.280 --> 00:40:05.760
the most important
relationship in his life.

00:40:05.760 --> 00:40:09.090
Hoover does not get married,
he does not have children,

00:40:09.090 --> 00:40:12.150
but he and Tolson really
relate to each other

00:40:12.150 --> 00:40:14.370
as a social couple in the way

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:16.650
that you might expect that spouses would.

00:40:16.650 --> 00:40:18.060
They not only work together,

00:40:18.060 --> 00:40:19.950
they have all of their meals together,

00:40:19.950 --> 00:40:23.370
they vacation together,
they socialize together,

00:40:23.370 --> 00:40:26.940
and they're pretty widely
accepted in Washington,

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:30.330
New York, Florida, California,
the places that they spend

00:40:30.330 --> 00:40:33.360
their time together as a social couple.

00:40:33.360 --> 00:40:36.510
So there are lots of nice
scenes in the book of, you know,

00:40:36.510 --> 00:40:41.253
Hoover and Clyde double dating
with say, Dick and Pat Nixon.

00:40:42.720 --> 00:40:46.920
And so Hoover's life, his
private life, his social life,

00:40:46.920 --> 00:40:49.140
his intimate life is, I would say,

00:40:49.140 --> 00:40:51.750
a funny combination of, on
the one hand being very,

00:40:51.750 --> 00:40:55.020
very public and open, and then of course,

00:40:55.020 --> 00:40:57.423
having lots of secrets as well.

00:40:59.040 --> 00:41:02.580
I'll show you a little bit
about Hoover and Tolson.

00:41:02.580 --> 00:41:06.270
So this is one of my favorite
pictures of them together.

00:41:06.270 --> 00:41:10.230
This is a celebration of
Tolson's 20 years at the FBI.

00:41:10.230 --> 00:41:12.060
I think it was his 20th, it
might have been his 15th,

00:41:12.060 --> 00:41:15.810
but one of Tolson's
anniversaries at the FBI,

00:41:15.810 --> 00:41:17.550
and what I like about this is not only

00:41:17.550 --> 00:41:18.870
that they're wearing matching suits

00:41:18.870 --> 00:41:20.190
and standing in a sea of flowers,

00:41:20.190 --> 00:41:23.430
but it looks kind of like
a wedding photo, right?

00:41:23.430 --> 00:41:26.220
But that's the closest we have to that.

00:41:26.220 --> 00:41:30.120
I think the more interesting
photos, and one of the places

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:31.770
that I really tried to access

00:41:31.770 --> 00:41:34.380
not only their public life
together, which as I said,

00:41:34.380 --> 00:41:36.030
is very open, right?

00:41:36.030 --> 00:41:38.970
I mean, they're Broadway
shows and nightclubs

00:41:38.970 --> 00:41:41.880
and vacations and all
over the place together,

00:41:41.880 --> 00:41:45.720
the horse track, the baseball game,

00:41:45.720 --> 00:41:48.420
but a little bit more
of their interiority.

00:41:48.420 --> 00:41:50.040
What did any of this mean to them?

00:41:50.040 --> 00:41:51.900
One of the best sources for that

00:41:51.900 --> 00:41:55.950
was Hoover's private photo album.

00:41:55.950 --> 00:41:58.590
I've reproduced some
of those photos there.

00:41:58.590 --> 00:42:01.110
But that photo album is really filled

00:42:01.110 --> 00:42:03.930
with fairly intimate portraits,

00:42:03.930 --> 00:42:05.790
particularly in the '30s and '40s,

00:42:05.790 --> 00:42:07.920
of their vacations together

00:42:07.920 --> 00:42:10.620
in places like Miami and California.

00:42:10.620 --> 00:42:13.980
So here's a shot
presumably taken by Tolson

00:42:13.980 --> 00:42:18.980
of Hoover at the beach, getting dressed.

00:42:19.080 --> 00:42:23.640
This is Tolson on a fishing
boat, a photo taken by Hoover.

00:42:23.640 --> 00:42:27.810
And there are dozens and dozens
of these photos, you know,

00:42:27.810 --> 00:42:32.040
really quite intimate photos,
of the two of them together.

00:42:32.040 --> 00:42:34.350
And there are other ways to I think,

00:42:34.350 --> 00:42:37.920
begin to get at what
they meant to each other.

00:42:37.920 --> 00:42:40.200
But these are some of my favorite sources.

00:42:40.200 --> 00:42:43.080
Of course then, in thinking
about Hoover and Tolson

00:42:43.080 --> 00:42:47.340
as a couple, there are of
a couple of complications.

00:42:47.340 --> 00:42:49.080
One, we don't know of course,

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:52.920
whether this was in fact a
sexual relationship or not.

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:57.660
Two, is that they came of age
and were deeply interested

00:42:57.660 --> 00:43:01.380
in and involved in policing the sex lives

00:43:01.380 --> 00:43:06.150
of other gay people, particularly
in the '40s, '50s and '60s

00:43:06.150 --> 00:43:09.510
when it was federal policy
that you could not be gay

00:43:09.510 --> 00:43:12.360
and be employed in the federal government.

00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:16.410
So Hoover is on the one hand,
involved in this very public,

00:43:16.410 --> 00:43:20.760
open, intimate social
partnership with his number two

00:43:20.760 --> 00:43:23.490
at the FBI, and then on the other hand,

00:43:23.490 --> 00:43:28.080
is actively policing the
sexual and private lives

00:43:28.080 --> 00:43:31.080
of thousands of federal civil servants.

00:43:31.080 --> 00:43:33.360
And to complicate matters
further, of course,

00:43:33.360 --> 00:43:36.690
when anyone suggested,
as many, many people did

00:43:36.690 --> 00:43:38.820
over the course of Hoover's life

00:43:38.820 --> 00:43:43.820
that perhaps Hoover himself
was gay or queer or homosexual,

00:43:44.160 --> 00:43:46.110
right, in the language of the time,

00:43:46.110 --> 00:43:48.990
they might actually get
an FBI agent showing up

00:43:48.990 --> 00:43:52.650
to their door, knocking on
it and saying, you know,

00:43:52.650 --> 00:43:54.480
we've heard you've been
spreading these rumors

00:43:54.480 --> 00:43:56.340
about the director of the FBI.

00:43:56.340 --> 00:43:58.860
You know, we're sure you don't
want to say such scurrilous

00:43:58.860 --> 00:44:00.690
things about the most
wonderful, important,

00:44:00.690 --> 00:44:02.310
federal servant we've ever seen.

00:44:02.310 --> 00:44:05.490
And of course, people
were a little frightened

00:44:05.490 --> 00:44:08.850
in those situations, but
he really used the power

00:44:08.850 --> 00:44:13.560
of the bureau that he created,
to police his own reputation

00:44:13.560 --> 00:44:17.460
and to police the
boundaries of what was known

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:20.610
about his own intimate life.

00:44:20.610 --> 00:44:23.550
So I'm happy to talk more
about that in the Q&amp;A.

00:44:23.550 --> 00:44:26.370
That relationship plays
a big role in the book.

00:44:26.370 --> 00:44:29.160
But I wanna say a few other
things about what happens

00:44:29.160 --> 00:44:34.160
in the '30s and '40s, as
Hoover is the head of the FBI.

00:44:34.920 --> 00:44:37.230
And probably the most surprising thing

00:44:37.230 --> 00:44:41.790
is that it is really during the New deal

00:44:41.790 --> 00:44:46.790
that he ends up acquiring most
of his power and influence.

00:44:47.640 --> 00:44:49.530
He spends a lot of the '20s and '30s,

00:44:49.530 --> 00:44:52.530
kind of engaged in building
up the scientific end

00:44:52.530 --> 00:44:54.963
of the bureau, the fingerprint division,

00:44:56.070 --> 00:44:58.410
getting control of
crime statistics, right?

00:44:58.410 --> 00:45:00.270
When you hear crime statistics today,

00:45:00.270 --> 00:45:03.300
that's because J. Edgar
Hoover in the '30s,

00:45:03.300 --> 00:45:06.810
demanded that the FBI be in
control of crime statistics.

00:45:06.810 --> 00:45:10.500
So if you hear the FBI
uniform crime reports today,

00:45:10.500 --> 00:45:12.480
that comes from Hoover's years

00:45:12.480 --> 00:45:15.870
setting up the FBI lab,
setting up its academy,

00:45:15.870 --> 00:45:18.690
setting up these new training facilities

00:45:18.690 --> 00:45:21.510
to professionalize the Bureau.

00:45:21.510 --> 00:45:25.830
During the 1920s, these are
the glamour shots of the FBI

00:45:25.830 --> 00:45:28.290
that Hoover likes to promote.

00:45:28.290 --> 00:45:30.330
There are sort of endless numbers of shots

00:45:30.330 --> 00:45:32.040
of just filing cabinets, right?

00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:36.360
Guys standing at filing
cabinets showing off

00:45:36.360 --> 00:45:38.970
how much they know that
they are, you know,

00:45:38.970 --> 00:45:41.130
white collar paper pushers, et cetera.

00:45:41.130 --> 00:45:45.390
But this is Hoover's
favorite image of the Bureau

00:45:45.390 --> 00:45:47.190
in the 1920s,

00:45:47.190 --> 00:45:49.980
and then suddenly in the mid 1930s,

00:45:49.980 --> 00:45:51.870
that all begins to change.

00:45:51.870 --> 00:45:56.130
By 1935 and 1936, you're
not seeing filing cabinets,

00:45:56.130 --> 00:46:00.210
but you're seeing the
famous images of FBI agents

00:46:00.210 --> 00:46:04.260
with Tommy guns, as great crime fighters.

00:46:04.260 --> 00:46:09.240
The main thing that happens in
the 1930s to produce the FBI

00:46:09.240 --> 00:46:12.570
that we know today, is
that Franklin Roosevelt

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:17.280
and the state builders of
the New Deal look to the FBI

00:46:17.280 --> 00:46:21.030
to become the sort of
federal standard bearers

00:46:21.030 --> 00:46:25.350
for what they describe as a
war on crime in the 1930s.

00:46:25.350 --> 00:46:27.990
So suddenly, Hoover's
nice white collar lawyers

00:46:27.990 --> 00:46:32.610
and accountants are told,
beginning in 1934 and '35,

00:46:32.610 --> 00:46:36.540
that they are actually going
to be gun-wielding policemen.

00:46:36.540 --> 00:46:39.780
Some of them have no
idea how to shoot a gun.

00:46:39.780 --> 00:46:43.080
They had no expectation that
this was going to be their job.

00:46:43.080 --> 00:46:48.080
But in the midst of a New
Deal push to go after a wave

00:46:48.900 --> 00:46:52.140
of kind of high-profile
kidnappers, bank robbers,

00:46:52.140 --> 00:46:53.760
figures like John Dillinger,

00:46:53.760 --> 00:46:56.460
you see a radical change at the FBI,

00:46:56.460 --> 00:46:59.370
and that's where we begin to
get this very different image

00:46:59.370 --> 00:47:02.370
and Hoover responds to
that crisis quickly.

00:47:02.370 --> 00:47:05.100
That happens through crime legislation,

00:47:05.100 --> 00:47:07.230
and as you can see through
some of these images,

00:47:07.230 --> 00:47:09.720
the FBI becomes essentially,

00:47:09.720 --> 00:47:12.360
a New Deal alphabet agency, right?

00:47:12.360 --> 00:47:16.740
They become symbols of these
new forms of federal power

00:47:16.740 --> 00:47:19.170
that are going to intervene,
not only in solving

00:47:19.170 --> 00:47:23.070
the economic oppressions and
problems of the New Deal,

00:47:23.070 --> 00:47:24.930
but are going to be much more involved

00:47:24.930 --> 00:47:27.000
in law enforcement as well.

00:47:27.000 --> 00:47:29.103
So here they are fighting kidnapping.

00:47:30.090 --> 00:47:34.650
Along the way, Hoover begins
to develop what becomes

00:47:34.650 --> 00:47:37.350
one of his signature
features as a bureaucrat,

00:47:37.350 --> 00:47:39.660
which is his public relations division,

00:47:39.660 --> 00:47:42.990
encouraged by Franklin
Roosevelt who says, you know,

00:47:42.990 --> 00:47:45.390
you can't just accept
that people understand

00:47:45.390 --> 00:47:46.380
the work of government.

00:47:46.380 --> 00:47:47.910
You've got to sell it to them.

00:47:47.910 --> 00:47:50.220
You've got to explain to them

00:47:50.220 --> 00:47:51.960
what the federal government is doing.

00:47:51.960 --> 00:47:55.710
Hoover becomes really,
a master of publicity,

00:47:55.710 --> 00:48:00.060
Hollywood movies, newspaper
reports, et cetera,

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:02.250
begin to glorify him and really turn him

00:48:02.250 --> 00:48:05.160
into a celebrity in the 1930s.

00:48:05.160 --> 00:48:08.010
And then the last thing
that happens in the 1930s

00:48:08.010 --> 00:48:10.470
and into the '40s is that the FBI moves

00:48:10.470 --> 00:48:12.750
much more aggressively into the world

00:48:12.750 --> 00:48:16.050
of domestic intelligence espionage,

00:48:16.050 --> 00:48:20.370
in ways that are fundamentally new.

00:48:20.370 --> 00:48:24.240
And it's under Franklin Roosevelt
that the FBI really begins

00:48:24.240 --> 00:48:28.200
to do the work that we
associate with it today,

00:48:28.200 --> 00:48:29.370
on the one hand,

00:48:29.370 --> 00:48:32.370
to be a federal criminal
law enforcement agency,

00:48:32.370 --> 00:48:33.570
and on the other hand,

00:48:33.570 --> 00:48:36.030
to be our domestic intelligence agency

00:48:36.030 --> 00:48:38.940
dealing with things like espionage,

00:48:38.940 --> 00:48:41.520
and in particular with
political intelligence,

00:48:41.520 --> 00:48:46.520
surveillance and kind of
mass political surveillance.

00:48:48.960 --> 00:48:52.680
The other thing that happens
by the end of the war

00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:56.220
is that the FBI has quadrupled in size.

00:48:56.220 --> 00:48:59.910
So Hoover started out in
the 1920s with this tiny

00:48:59.910 --> 00:49:02.340
little group of a few hundred agents,

00:49:02.340 --> 00:49:05.310
a few hundred people in
the fingerprint division.

00:49:05.310 --> 00:49:09.570
This is his filing cabinets
by the end of the war.

00:49:09.570 --> 00:49:11.670
This is the Washington Armory,

00:49:11.670 --> 00:49:15.540
the FBI moves in, and
as you can see, he now,

00:49:15.540 --> 00:49:18.873
by the end of the war, really
has an army of his own.

00:49:19.980 --> 00:49:22.260
So that is part two of the book,

00:49:22.260 --> 00:49:24.480
this period of institution building

00:49:24.480 --> 00:49:27.630
that moves from the '20s through the '40s.

00:49:27.630 --> 00:49:31.320
I'll just say a few words
about the last two sections

00:49:31.320 --> 00:49:34.080
of the book, which I think
in many ways are the periods

00:49:34.080 --> 00:49:36.840
that most people today know best

00:49:36.840 --> 00:49:38.250
when they think about Hoover,

00:49:38.250 --> 00:49:41.433
when they think about
the FBI and its history.

00:49:42.900 --> 00:49:45.960
The first of those is the
period in the late '40s

00:49:45.960 --> 00:49:50.400
and into the 1950s, when I
would say Hoover has finished

00:49:50.400 --> 00:49:54.210
a lot of building the FBI
and then puts it to work

00:49:54.210 --> 00:49:58.020
for the causes that he cares
about most and above all,

00:49:58.020 --> 00:50:02.130
puts them to work in the
cause of fighting communism.

00:50:02.130 --> 00:50:03.780
They've been doing some of that work

00:50:03.780 --> 00:50:08.280
in the '40s during the war,
but as the war comes to an end,

00:50:08.280 --> 00:50:11.100
the Cold War begins to emerge.

00:50:11.100 --> 00:50:15.930
Hoover very quickly becomes one
of the most prominent voices

00:50:15.930 --> 00:50:18.390
of anti-communism in the United States.

00:50:18.390 --> 00:50:21.750
This is the cover of "Newsweek", by 1947,

00:50:21.750 --> 00:50:26.100
already looking to Hoover
as a national spokesman

00:50:26.100 --> 00:50:28.950
for the anti-communist cause and really,

00:50:28.950 --> 00:50:32.430
for trying to raise the level of alarm

00:50:32.430 --> 00:50:36.630
in the United States around
the question of communism.

00:50:36.630 --> 00:50:38.910
I think there are a couple of
things that are worth noting

00:50:38.910 --> 00:50:43.110
about the way that Hoover
relates to this question

00:50:43.110 --> 00:50:45.630
and why he's really important.

00:50:45.630 --> 00:50:47.910
I said in the '20s, '30s, through the war,

00:50:47.910 --> 00:50:49.920
he's really building the Bureau.

00:50:49.920 --> 00:50:53.920
He is not doing, I think, as much

00:50:55.140 --> 00:50:58.590
following of his own
agenda, as we begin to see

00:50:58.590 --> 00:51:01.470
during this period when
he's really secured

00:51:01.470 --> 00:51:03.750
the institutional heft of the FBI,

00:51:03.750 --> 00:51:06.060
has secured his own public profile,

00:51:06.060 --> 00:51:10.290
and then can really begin
to shape public opinion

00:51:10.290 --> 00:51:12.360
and to shape Washington politics

00:51:12.360 --> 00:51:14.763
in much more autonomous ways.

00:51:16.080 --> 00:51:21.080
It is possible to imagine an
FBI director in the 1940s,

00:51:21.180 --> 00:51:24.330
you know, faced with
the emerging Cold War,

00:51:24.330 --> 00:51:28.320
who takes a pretty narrow view
of national security, right,

00:51:28.320 --> 00:51:32.520
says that I am, you know, a
federal law enforcement agent

00:51:32.520 --> 00:51:36.510
and the head of our
domestic political police,

00:51:36.510 --> 00:51:37.980
but in that role,

00:51:37.980 --> 00:51:41.970
my job is to look into
questions of espionage,

00:51:41.970 --> 00:51:44.880
perhaps to keep an eye
on the communist party,

00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:46.170
which is in fact, you know,

00:51:46.170 --> 00:51:49.890
cooperating with Soviet
agents during these years,

00:51:49.890 --> 00:51:52.290
both around espionage and is taking money

00:51:52.290 --> 00:51:55.950
from the Soviet Union, and
that's where my job ends, right?

00:51:55.950 --> 00:51:57.840
That's what a national security

00:51:57.840 --> 00:52:00.720
and law enforcement profile looks like.

00:52:00.720 --> 00:52:02.610
Hoover sort of does the opposite.

00:52:02.610 --> 00:52:03.990
He does that kind of work,

00:52:03.990 --> 00:52:06.150
and there's lots of
interesting stuff to say

00:52:06.150 --> 00:52:09.750
about the big espionage
cases of those years,

00:52:09.750 --> 00:52:14.310
but he makes himself a
kind of cultural icon,

00:52:14.310 --> 00:52:17.430
a political force in defining the struggle

00:52:17.430 --> 00:52:21.150
against communism in the
broadest ways possible.

00:52:21.150 --> 00:52:26.150
So to Hoover, you know, it is
not only a matter of espionage

00:52:26.370 --> 00:52:29.490
or of containing the role
of the communist party,

00:52:29.490 --> 00:52:32.160
but he really frames anti-communism

00:52:32.160 --> 00:52:36.650
as an existential struggle
that involves every person

00:52:36.650 --> 00:52:40.380
in the United States, and that
is constantly being fought

00:52:40.380 --> 00:52:43.440
out in every institution
in the United States,

00:52:43.440 --> 00:52:46.530
from universities to public schools,

00:52:46.530 --> 00:52:49.050
to churches, to the government itself,

00:52:49.050 --> 00:52:52.230
and that involves every
aspect of people's lives,

00:52:52.230 --> 00:52:56.100
from race relations to religion,

00:52:56.100 --> 00:52:59.100
to family structure, and on and on.

00:52:59.100 --> 00:53:01.290
And so he's engaged in a lot

00:53:01.290 --> 00:53:05.100
of very kind of technical
investigations on the one hand,

00:53:05.100 --> 00:53:09.210
and then on the other hand,
he's pouring out a whole series

00:53:09.210 --> 00:53:14.210
of books, articles, films,
framing the communist question

00:53:14.580 --> 00:53:19.580
in very dramatic, and as I
said, sort of existential ways.

00:53:19.620 --> 00:53:22.620
Couple of other things to
note about this period.

00:53:22.620 --> 00:53:24.750
One is that it's a period when he begins

00:53:24.750 --> 00:53:28.050
to work very closely with
congressional committees,

00:53:28.050 --> 00:53:30.900
especially committees
like the House Committee

00:53:30.900 --> 00:53:32.910
on American Activities,

00:53:32.910 --> 00:53:35.583
the Senate Internal Security Subcommittee,

00:53:36.870 --> 00:53:38.580
congressional committees that are involved

00:53:38.580 --> 00:53:41.490
in the kind of work that he cares about.

00:53:41.490 --> 00:53:43.620
One of my favorite chapters in the book

00:53:43.620 --> 00:53:47.400
is about his starting in the 1940s,

00:53:47.400 --> 00:53:51.360
as a variety of these committees
need professional staff,

00:53:51.360 --> 00:53:54.960
to agree to start staffing
them with FBI agents.

00:53:54.960 --> 00:53:59.960
And so he gets this very
dramatic inroad into Congress.

00:54:00.060 --> 00:54:02.370
The Appropriations Committee, HUAC,

00:54:02.370 --> 00:54:06.150
a whole series of committees
end up staffed by FBI agents,

00:54:06.150 --> 00:54:09.120
which means he has access
to lots of information,

00:54:09.120 --> 00:54:13.800
lots of influence, and lots
of important ties to Congress,

00:54:13.800 --> 00:54:17.520
just one symbol of his bureaucratic skill.

00:54:17.520 --> 00:54:22.020
This is Hoover testifying
in front of Congress in 1953

00:54:22.020 --> 00:54:25.410
in the Harry Dexter White spy case.

00:54:25.410 --> 00:54:29.880
The other is his relationship
to Joseph McCarthy.

00:54:29.880 --> 00:54:33.480
So this is a photo of a vacation photo

00:54:33.480 --> 00:54:35.580
with Hoover and Tolson.

00:54:35.580 --> 00:54:38.880
Let's see, Hoover's over here on my left.

00:54:38.880 --> 00:54:41.910
Tolson is the second one
from the right there,

00:54:41.910 --> 00:54:44.070
and that's Joe McCarthy on the end.

00:54:44.070 --> 00:54:48.330
So they're all vacationing
together out in California.

00:54:48.330 --> 00:54:50.250
In some ways they are social friends,

00:54:50.250 --> 00:54:55.250
they obviously share a set of
anti-communist commitments,

00:54:55.470 --> 00:54:58.350
but what was more interesting
to me were the ways

00:54:58.350 --> 00:55:02.250
in which Hoover and
McCarthy ultimately diverge.

00:55:02.250 --> 00:55:06.390
Hoover becomes seen
popularly in the White House,

00:55:06.390 --> 00:55:09.000
as well as among liberals,

00:55:09.000 --> 00:55:13.170
as the more responsible
alternative to Joe McCarthy,

00:55:13.170 --> 00:55:16.290
who ends up depicted as
the kind of demagogue,

00:55:16.290 --> 00:55:18.540
the loose cannon, the liar.

00:55:18.540 --> 00:55:21.750
So in our minds, I think
these two were often

00:55:21.750 --> 00:55:23.310
kind of conflated together,

00:55:23.310 --> 00:55:25.920
but in the politics of their moment,

00:55:25.920 --> 00:55:29.190
they were seen as very
different sorts of figures.

00:55:29.190 --> 00:55:32.430
And in fact, it's Hoover
that many liberals end up

00:55:32.430 --> 00:55:37.173
rallying behind as sort of an
alternative to Joe McCarthy.

00:55:38.250 --> 00:55:40.560
I'll just show a few examples,

00:55:40.560 --> 00:55:44.100
as well of Hoover's
broader cultural impact.

00:55:44.100 --> 00:55:47.910
So this is, you know, a bus bench.

00:55:47.910 --> 00:55:50.580
I think it's in Houston, is
that what it says up there?

00:55:50.580 --> 00:55:53.850
At any rate, I said
that Hoover was not only

00:55:53.850 --> 00:55:57.330
this kind of technocrat,
but this significant popular

00:55:57.330 --> 00:55:59.430
and cultural figure.

00:55:59.430 --> 00:56:02.340
This is him fighting communism.

00:56:02.340 --> 00:56:05.130
He wrote a bestseller
called, "Masters of Deceit"

00:56:05.130 --> 00:56:07.650
that many people were forced
to read as young children,

00:56:07.650 --> 00:56:09.450
telling you how you could participate

00:56:09.450 --> 00:56:11.460
in the fight against communism.

00:56:11.460 --> 00:56:14.280
This is Hoover lecturing American families

00:56:14.280 --> 00:56:16.260
about how to raise their
children, which, you know,

00:56:16.260 --> 00:56:18.300
people would occasionally
point out they're not sure

00:56:18.300 --> 00:56:20.910
where he gets his
expertise on that question.

00:56:20.910 --> 00:56:23.370
And then finally, in the '50s, as I said,

00:56:23.370 --> 00:56:28.370
Hoover's this important cultural
figure on anti-communism,

00:56:28.380 --> 00:56:31.980
but he also becomes a very sort
of outspoken religious voice

00:56:31.980 --> 00:56:36.240
and plays a big role in the
kind of surge of religiosity

00:56:36.240 --> 00:56:39.220
that's so important to
the conservative politics

00:56:40.056 --> 00:56:41.760
of the 1950s.

00:56:41.760 --> 00:56:44.250
All right, I will finish up very quickly,

00:56:44.250 --> 00:56:47.610
just with the period of the
'60s and '70s, which as I said,

00:56:47.610 --> 00:56:50.850
I think are the most familiar
to many people today,

00:56:50.850 --> 00:56:54.663
and really are the period
when Hoover becomes,

00:56:55.500 --> 00:56:57.570
if not a less influential figure,

00:56:57.570 --> 00:57:00.240
a less universally popular figure.

00:57:00.240 --> 00:57:03.510
By the late 1950s, his popularity ratings

00:57:03.510 --> 00:57:07.170
are in the 70s, 80s, 90 percentiles.

00:57:07.170 --> 00:57:10.560
That begins to fall off a
little bit in the 1960s,

00:57:10.560 --> 00:57:14.733
as the country's politics
change and as he ages.

00:57:15.900 --> 00:57:17.550
So the last part of the book

00:57:17.550 --> 00:57:21.660
is this period from 1960
up to his death in 1972.

00:57:22.680 --> 00:57:26.130
That covers what I think, are
three of the most interesting

00:57:26.130 --> 00:57:29.190
presidential relationships in the book.

00:57:29.190 --> 00:57:30.840
The first is his relationship

00:57:30.840 --> 00:57:32.460
with the Kennedy administration.

00:57:32.460 --> 00:57:34.650
And what I love about this picture

00:57:34.650 --> 00:57:37.410
is that I think it sort of
captures that relationship,

00:57:37.410 --> 00:57:39.420
which it was not a happy one.

00:57:39.420 --> 00:57:41.520
They really didn't like each other.

00:57:41.520 --> 00:57:43.980
And John Kennedy is one of those figures

00:57:43.980 --> 00:57:46.830
who might have fired Hoover.

00:57:46.830 --> 00:57:48.690
He certainly thought about it.

00:57:48.690 --> 00:57:52.020
He considered it and thought
that it was not a good idea

00:57:52.020 --> 00:57:54.150
for a variety of reasons.

00:57:54.150 --> 00:57:58.530
Hoover was particularly
hostile to Bobby Kennedy,

00:57:58.530 --> 00:58:00.870
who of course was made Attorney General

00:58:00.870 --> 00:58:04.590
under his brother's presidency
at a fairly young age.

00:58:04.590 --> 00:58:08.040
I found myself actually
surprisingly sympathetic to Hoover,

00:58:08.040 --> 00:58:10.380
who is in his early 60s, that is saying,

00:58:10.380 --> 00:58:12.900
why is there this 35 year
old attorney general?

00:58:12.900 --> 00:58:16.650
He doesn't seem very qualified,
and I don't really like him.

00:58:16.650 --> 00:58:18.810
But at any rate,

00:58:18.810 --> 00:58:21.840
theirs is an interesting
relationship as well.

00:58:21.840 --> 00:58:23.970
It then goes on to Hoover's relationship

00:58:23.970 --> 00:58:28.650
with Lyndon Johnson, and I
think we would tend to think

00:58:28.650 --> 00:58:32.550
of Hoover and Johnson as
being on opposite sides

00:58:32.550 --> 00:58:36.450
of things like civil rights,

00:58:36.450 --> 00:58:41.450
other major political trends of the 1960s,

00:58:41.460 --> 00:58:43.440
Johnson as a great liberal icon,

00:58:43.440 --> 00:58:48.000
Hoover increasingly can seen
as a far right conservative,

00:58:48.000 --> 00:58:50.610
but actually they got along really well

00:58:50.610 --> 00:58:53.250
and they cooperated in pretty dramatic

00:58:53.250 --> 00:58:57.570
and often quite outrageous
ways in the 1960s.

00:58:57.570 --> 00:59:01.080
They had been neighbors on
the same block in Washington,

00:59:01.080 --> 00:59:04.230
so they knew each other
from the 1940s onward,

00:59:04.230 --> 00:59:07.950
and it's really Lyndon Johnson
that we can thank or blame

00:59:07.950 --> 00:59:10.440
for making J. Edgar Hoover,

00:59:10.440 --> 00:59:13.950
essentially FBI Director for life.

00:59:13.950 --> 00:59:16.620
Had things gone their normal course,

00:59:16.620 --> 00:59:20.040
Hoover would've been forced
to retire at the age of 70.

00:59:20.040 --> 00:59:24.660
That would've been January 1st, 1965.

00:59:24.660 --> 00:59:28.170
But Lyndon Johnson decided to exempt him

00:59:28.170 --> 00:59:30.780
from the mandatory federal retirement age.

00:59:30.780 --> 00:59:34.140
And this is the ceremony in 1964,

00:59:34.140 --> 00:59:37.860
where Johnson says that he
wants Hoover to stay on.

00:59:37.860 --> 00:59:41.103
Now, why did Johnson
want Hoover to stay on?

00:59:42.390 --> 00:59:45.480
It's an interesting
question, but as I said,

00:59:45.480 --> 00:59:48.360
they had this longstanding relationship,

00:59:48.360 --> 00:59:51.720
and also Johnson went on to use Hoover

00:59:51.720 --> 00:59:54.120
and the FBI more aggressively

00:59:54.120 --> 00:59:56.370
than almost any other president.

00:59:56.370 --> 00:59:58.740
Sometimes that was in the service

00:59:58.740 --> 01:00:00.870
of civil rights enforcement.

01:00:00.870 --> 01:00:04.230
Johnson really pushed the
FBI to be more aggressive

01:00:04.230 --> 01:00:06.240
about civil rights enforcement,

01:00:06.240 --> 01:00:11.240
particularly after the civil
rights legislation of that era.

01:00:11.490 --> 01:00:15.090
But he also used the
FBI pretty aggressively

01:00:15.090 --> 01:00:18.900
to police the civil rights
movement, to police the left.

01:00:18.900 --> 01:00:21.960
One of the more outrageous
moments is when Johnson

01:00:21.960 --> 01:00:26.960
asks the FBI to conduct
surveillance wiretapping

01:00:27.300 --> 01:00:30.330
infiltration of civil rights demonstrators

01:00:30.330 --> 01:00:35.330
at the 1964 Democratic National
Convention on his behalf

01:00:35.340 --> 01:00:38.670
and to report back to him
what was going on there

01:00:38.670 --> 01:00:42.540
with the Mississippi
Freedom Democratic Party.

01:00:42.540 --> 01:00:44.850
At any rate, there's a lot
about Hoover and Johnson.

01:00:44.850 --> 01:00:46.920
It's a really interesting, complicated,

01:00:46.920 --> 01:00:49.830
and I think counterintuitive
relationship in many ways.

01:00:49.830 --> 01:00:52.950
And there's a lot about
Richard Nixon as well,

01:00:52.950 --> 01:00:56.460
which is probably the most long-standing

01:00:56.460 --> 01:01:00.420
and closest presidential
relationship of Hoover's life.

01:01:00.420 --> 01:01:04.560
They too, had been very
close since the 1940s.

01:01:04.560 --> 01:01:09.420
This is them vacationing
together, as well in the 1950s,

01:01:09.420 --> 01:01:10.950
but they run into some conflicts

01:01:10.950 --> 01:01:15.780
when Nixon becomes president
and theirs is an interesting

01:01:15.780 --> 01:01:18.810
and complicated relationship as well.

01:01:18.810 --> 01:01:21.990
That section of the book also
goes into a lot of the big

01:01:21.990 --> 01:01:23.970
investigations of the era,

01:01:23.970 --> 01:01:26.280
most notably the Kennedy assassination

01:01:26.280 --> 01:01:28.410
and the King assassination.

01:01:28.410 --> 01:01:31.620
I should say, in writing the
book, there were a few sections

01:01:31.620 --> 01:01:34.710
that I really dreaded having to get into.

01:01:34.710 --> 01:01:38.820
Those were among them because
the volume of material

01:01:38.820 --> 01:01:41.850
related to those cases is so vast.

01:01:41.850 --> 01:01:44.760
The literature related
to those cases is so vast

01:01:44.760 --> 01:01:48.570
and so much of it is so
bizarre and terrible,

01:01:48.570 --> 01:01:50.760
and I had to wade into that.

01:01:50.760 --> 01:01:53.220
But the real thrust of this final section

01:01:53.220 --> 01:01:58.220
is about Hoover's political
policing in the 1960s,

01:01:58.710 --> 01:02:01.500
particularly the FBI's campaigns

01:02:01.500 --> 01:02:05.430
against the civil rights
movement, against the new left,

01:02:05.430 --> 01:02:09.180
and against figures
like Martin Luther King,

01:02:09.180 --> 01:02:12.000
in which the FBI conducted not only

01:02:12.000 --> 01:02:16.770
a pretty aggressive public
campaign to discredit King

01:02:16.770 --> 01:02:20.550
and destabilize the movement,
but also a whole series

01:02:20.550 --> 01:02:24.240
of pretty outrageous secret activities,

01:02:24.240 --> 01:02:29.240
including wiretapping King's
home and office phones,

01:02:29.340 --> 01:02:34.080
planting bugs in his hotel
rooms to record his sex life,

01:02:34.080 --> 01:02:36.330
sending those recordings to King,

01:02:36.330 --> 01:02:40.350
along with anonymous threatening notes,

01:02:40.350 --> 01:02:44.220
and deploying those sorts of
tactics, not only against King,

01:02:44.220 --> 01:02:48.750
but against a wide variety
of figures on the left,

01:02:48.750 --> 01:02:53.220
as well as to some degree,
on the right as well.

01:02:53.220 --> 01:02:57.990
Probably the most famous of
those programs in the 1960s

01:02:57.990 --> 01:03:00.783
is a program known as COINTELPRO,

01:03:02.070 --> 01:03:04.620
which was started in the
late '50s as a program

01:03:04.620 --> 01:03:06.720
aimed at the Communist Party,

01:03:06.720 --> 01:03:09.810
expanded over the course of the 1960s,

01:03:09.810 --> 01:03:13.740
to become a program of
surveillance and disruption

01:03:13.740 --> 01:03:17.430
aimed at nearly every
organization on the left,

01:03:17.430 --> 01:03:20.370
from the civil rights movement
to the Black Panthers,

01:03:20.370 --> 01:03:22.440
to student activists.

01:03:22.440 --> 01:03:25.740
It was a program that involved
not only surveillance,

01:03:25.740 --> 01:03:30.240
wire tapping, et cetera, but
active disruptive techniques,

01:03:30.240 --> 01:03:33.060
sending in informants to disrupt meetings,

01:03:33.060 --> 01:03:35.580
planting false newspaper allegations,

01:03:35.580 --> 01:03:37.860
working with local police

01:03:37.860 --> 01:03:42.860
to set up activists for arrest, et cetera.

01:03:42.990 --> 01:03:45.960
It's worth noting here
that most of this was aimed

01:03:45.960 --> 01:03:50.010
at the politics of the
left, but the FBI did employ

01:03:50.010 --> 01:03:53.190
some of those techniques
against the Ku Klux Klan,

01:03:53.190 --> 01:03:55.020
against neo-Nazi organizations,

01:03:55.020 --> 01:03:59.400
white supremacist
organizations in the 1960s.

01:03:59.400 --> 01:04:03.510
So I think even in this period, you know,

01:04:03.510 --> 01:04:08.510
Hoover has a more complicated
story than is often portrayed

01:04:08.940 --> 01:04:12.960
in the public discourse,
although, you know,

01:04:12.960 --> 01:04:17.850
the basic thrust of COINTELPRO
as a pretty destructive,

01:04:17.850 --> 01:04:22.350
outrageous, and illegal program
that had a dramatic effect

01:04:22.350 --> 01:04:25.050
on the civil rights
movement in particular,

01:04:25.050 --> 01:04:29.193
but the new left more broadly,
I think is basically correct.

01:04:30.180 --> 01:04:35.180
And then we end up back here
in 1972 with Hoover's funeral.

01:04:36.600 --> 01:04:40.470
As I said, he sort of
died with his boots on

01:04:40.470 --> 01:04:45.300
as FBI director in May of 1972.

01:04:45.300 --> 01:04:47.970
And at the moment that he died,

01:04:47.970 --> 01:04:52.970
there was a lot of celebration
among those who hated him.

01:04:54.060 --> 01:04:56.610
But I would say more broadly, you know,

01:04:56.610 --> 01:05:00.240
reflection on the fact
that whatever you thought

01:05:00.240 --> 01:05:02.010
of what he had accomplished,

01:05:02.010 --> 01:05:06.240
he had been really a central
figure in the politics

01:05:06.240 --> 01:05:10.560
of the 20th century, a
central figure in the politics

01:05:10.560 --> 01:05:13.230
of Washington over that time period,

01:05:13.230 --> 01:05:17.910
and as Richard Nixon said
in his eulogy for Hoover,

01:05:17.910 --> 01:05:20.880
one of the giants of American history.

01:05:20.880 --> 01:05:25.880
And so my goal in writing this
book was to sort of take him

01:05:26.550 --> 01:05:30.030
from what I think he had
become in the 21st century,

01:05:30.030 --> 01:05:33.510
which is sort of a
one-dimensional caricature

01:05:33.510 --> 01:05:38.433
and bring him back into the
center of this politics,

01:05:40.230 --> 01:05:43.050
where he had his hands in so much,

01:05:43.050 --> 01:05:45.870
but where he wasn't
just acting on his own,

01:05:45.870 --> 01:05:48.900
he was acting in concert with many figures

01:05:48.900 --> 01:05:51.840
in the White House, with
many figures in Congress,

01:05:51.840 --> 01:05:55.200
and was an enormously kind of popular

01:05:55.200 --> 01:05:59.313
and influential public servant,
for better or for worse.

01:06:00.180 --> 01:06:01.830
So I will leave my remarks there.

01:06:01.830 --> 01:06:04.410
I'm happy to, you know, take questions

01:06:04.410 --> 01:06:06.480
in whatever form you think makes sense,

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:07.313
Ty?

01:06:07.313 --> 01:06:08.490
- Yeah, sure.

01:06:08.490 --> 01:06:10.184
Well, first of all...

01:06:10.184 --> 01:06:11.070
(applause)

01:06:11.070 --> 01:06:11.903
- Thank you.

01:06:13.830 --> 01:06:15.843
- Wonderful talk on a great subject.

01:06:18.000 --> 01:06:21.527
I just finished rereading "1984".

01:06:21.527 --> 01:06:24.420
COINTELPRO, by the way,
is a great new speak word.

01:06:24.420 --> 01:06:25.830
First to me, right.

01:06:25.830 --> 01:06:27.423
Maybe that wasn't lost on Hoover,

01:06:28.740 --> 01:06:32.580
and of course, Orwell is
preoccupied with the idea

01:06:32.580 --> 01:06:36.150
that technological
advancements would be developed

01:06:36.150 --> 01:06:38.640
and employed in this surveillance

01:06:38.640 --> 01:06:42.810
and the advancement of
surveillance of individual citizens

01:06:42.810 --> 01:06:45.870
and like the tele screens novel.

01:06:45.870 --> 01:06:50.870
So you talked a bit about
Hoover's ideas on science,

01:06:51.300 --> 01:06:52.550
which you described as...

01:06:54.270 --> 01:06:56.370
What were they, could you give us an idea,

01:06:56.370 --> 01:07:00.520
what they were and how
did they figure into

01:07:03.685 --> 01:07:06.950
for surveillance and his work at the FBI?

01:07:07.830 --> 01:07:09.490
- Yeah, great.

01:07:09.490 --> 01:07:10.530
That's a really important question,

01:07:10.530 --> 01:07:12.390
and it's a big theme of his career.

01:07:12.390 --> 01:07:16.560
So when he became director in the 1920s,

01:07:16.560 --> 01:07:20.430
he advertised himself as
kind of one of the great

01:07:20.430 --> 01:07:24.060
expositors of something
called scientific policing.

01:07:24.060 --> 01:07:29.060
And scientific policing
was supposed to make police

01:07:29.700 --> 01:07:33.030
more effective, and as
Hoover would've described it,

01:07:33.030 --> 01:07:36.150
less brutal at the same time.

01:07:36.150 --> 01:07:39.360
So what scientific policing really meant

01:07:39.360 --> 01:07:40.890
was a couple of things.

01:07:40.890 --> 01:07:45.890
One was harnessing a new
set of social science tools

01:07:45.930 --> 01:07:49.200
that could be put to the
work of law enforcement.

01:07:49.200 --> 01:07:51.750
So that was everything
from new classification

01:07:51.750 --> 01:07:56.400
and filing systems to
things like fingerprints,

01:07:56.400 --> 01:08:01.400
which are just coming in
as a form of identification

01:08:03.420 --> 01:08:05.550
to crime statistics.

01:08:05.550 --> 01:08:07.500
Right, there was no, I mean,

01:08:07.500 --> 01:08:09.930
in the 1920s into the early '30s,

01:08:09.930 --> 01:08:11.550
there's a lot of talk about this

01:08:11.550 --> 01:08:13.860
as a crisis in the United States,

01:08:13.860 --> 01:08:17.490
but there were no
national crime statistics

01:08:17.490 --> 01:08:19.440
being gathered in any effective way.

01:08:19.440 --> 01:08:24.440
So you had perceptions of
growing forms of crime.

01:08:24.690 --> 01:08:29.250
You have new things like the
automobile and the machine gun

01:08:29.250 --> 01:08:32.250
coming in as tools that
are allowing first,

01:08:32.250 --> 01:08:34.560
greater degrees of violence in some ways,

01:08:34.560 --> 01:08:38.010
but are allowing criminals
to cross federal lines,

01:08:38.010 --> 01:08:39.573
cross state lines.

01:08:40.410 --> 01:08:43.050
And so there are all sorts
of technological changes

01:08:43.050 --> 01:08:46.710
that are then driving this
push for crime statistics.

01:08:46.710 --> 01:08:50.730
So Hoover is very serious
about wanting to get control

01:08:50.730 --> 01:08:54.480
of crime statistics because he
saw it as one of these places

01:08:54.480 --> 01:08:57.300
where the FBI could be effective

01:08:57.300 --> 01:09:00.000
as a rather small organization.

01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:03.480
It could be in touch with
every police department

01:09:03.480 --> 01:09:06.810
in the country in order to
collect these statistics.

01:09:06.810 --> 01:09:08.670
And so that was going to be important

01:09:08.670 --> 01:09:11.340
for his kind of institutional influence.

01:09:11.340 --> 01:09:14.550
And then it was gonna
allow him to influence

01:09:14.550 --> 01:09:18.360
and define what crime
was, how it was counted,

01:09:18.360 --> 01:09:20.820
as well as what public
perceptions would be.

01:09:20.820 --> 01:09:23.100
So scientific policing is on the one hand,

01:09:23.100 --> 01:09:26.100
this set of kind of professionalizing

01:09:26.100 --> 01:09:30.360
and social science tools that
includes the FBI Academy,

01:09:30.360 --> 01:09:33.120
and then on the other hand,
there's this whole world

01:09:33.120 --> 01:09:36.600
of forensic science that is
just starting to open up.

01:09:36.600 --> 01:09:40.770
So fingerprints fit over
there as well a little bit.

01:09:40.770 --> 01:09:42.960
But one of his signature developments

01:09:42.960 --> 01:09:45.630
in the early '30s is the FBI crime lab,

01:09:45.630 --> 01:09:49.590
which is still the FBI crime
lab that we know today,

01:09:49.590 --> 01:09:52.380
which has had its own sort of failures

01:09:52.380 --> 01:09:55.200
and scandals over the last 20 years,

01:09:55.200 --> 01:09:58.140
but his vision was that
he was gonna employ,

01:09:58.140 --> 01:10:01.830
you know, a bunch of
chemists and scientists

01:10:01.830 --> 01:10:06.420
who would be able to be the
nation's top forensic experts,

01:10:06.420 --> 01:10:08.850
so that when local
police ran into problems,

01:10:08.850 --> 01:10:10.680
they could send their
evidence into the FBI,

01:10:10.680 --> 01:10:15.000
the FBI could help them to
figure out what was going on,

01:10:15.000 --> 01:10:18.060
and they would get that
information back down

01:10:18.060 --> 01:10:20.010
to the local police level.

01:10:20.010 --> 01:10:24.240
Because of course, in our
country, almost all to this day,

01:10:24.240 --> 01:10:27.720
almost all law enforcement
is done at the local level,

01:10:27.720 --> 01:10:31.230
and so to be a federal
law enforcement agency

01:10:31.230 --> 01:10:33.270
or even a federal intelligence agency,

01:10:33.270 --> 01:10:35.400
but in law enforcement in particular,

01:10:35.400 --> 01:10:37.170
Hoover always had this problem,

01:10:37.170 --> 01:10:40.740
which is that relative to
the scope of the country,

01:10:40.740 --> 01:10:42.900
the FBI was tiny,

01:10:42.900 --> 01:10:46.740
and that it didn't have
actually a lot of jurisdiction,

01:10:46.740 --> 01:10:49.230
or it had very weird
areas of jurisdiction,

01:10:49.230 --> 01:10:51.033
and that is still true today.

01:10:52.410 --> 01:10:56.370
And so he saw these not only as places

01:10:56.370 --> 01:10:58.470
of kind of scientific progress,

01:10:58.470 --> 01:11:01.200
but he also saw them as
sort of strategic points

01:11:01.200 --> 01:11:04.860
of intervention, a role
that the Bureau could play.

01:11:04.860 --> 01:11:06.720
And I think it's still
a really important part

01:11:06.720 --> 01:11:10.080
of the FBI's image, this
kind of, you know, I mean,

01:11:10.080 --> 01:11:12.330
how many television shows do we still have

01:11:12.330 --> 01:11:15.420
that are about you know,
kind of elite forensics labs

01:11:15.420 --> 01:11:17.940
that are often at the federal level,

01:11:17.940 --> 01:11:20.703
that are doing the work
that Hoover envisioned.

01:11:21.960 --> 01:11:24.810
- [Speaker] Did Hoover
seem to run off the rails

01:11:24.810 --> 01:11:25.923
in the '60s?

01:11:26.760 --> 01:11:30.690
And do you think that the student protests

01:11:30.690 --> 01:11:32.140
had anything to do with that?

01:11:33.690 --> 01:11:35.970
- Yeah, the '60s were
definitely the period

01:11:35.970 --> 01:11:39.600
in which I think what he
had built up in the '50s,

01:11:39.600 --> 01:11:41.910
which was very bipartisan support

01:11:41.910 --> 01:11:44.760
and very widespread public support,

01:11:44.760 --> 01:11:46.110
really begins to fracture.

01:11:46.110 --> 01:11:48.540
And a lot of it has to do with, you know,

01:11:48.540 --> 01:11:51.030
his attitudes toward and public campaigns

01:11:51.030 --> 01:11:54.210
against student activists,
the civil rights movement.

01:11:54.210 --> 01:11:58.050
Right, I think he took a
lot of what he had done,

01:11:58.050 --> 01:12:01.890
both in terms of formal techniques
and in terms of publicity

01:12:01.890 --> 01:12:05.340
with the communist party in the '50s,

01:12:05.340 --> 01:12:08.550
and kind of transferred
that over to the new left

01:12:08.550 --> 01:12:11.040
into the social movements of the '60s.

01:12:11.040 --> 01:12:14.370
But of course, the communist
party wasn't very popular

01:12:14.370 --> 01:12:16.980
in the 1950s, whereas those movements

01:12:16.980 --> 01:12:21.150
actually had real popular constituencies,

01:12:21.150 --> 01:12:26.150
a lot of, you know, a lot of
people who supported them,

01:12:26.430 --> 01:12:29.910
and so it didn't, his
formula suddenly didn't work

01:12:29.910 --> 01:12:30.870
in the 1960s.

01:12:30.870 --> 01:12:33.540
So some of that I think,
was generational, right?

01:12:33.540 --> 01:12:34.920
He was getting much older.

01:12:34.920 --> 01:12:37.380
He seemed much more sort of out of touch

01:12:37.380 --> 01:12:39.450
with the new generations of students.

01:12:39.450 --> 01:12:43.080
Some of it was that he
sort of couldn't get out

01:12:43.080 --> 01:12:46.230
of a sort of Cold War mentality.

01:12:46.230 --> 01:12:48.390
So he is constantly
accusing Martin Luther King

01:12:48.390 --> 01:12:51.240
of being a communist,
accusing student activists

01:12:51.240 --> 01:12:53.130
of being tools of the communists, right,

01:12:53.130 --> 01:12:54.210
the anti-war movement,

01:12:54.210 --> 01:12:56.640
and it didn't work as well in the '60s.

01:12:56.640 --> 01:12:59.820
And then I think there was
also just a sort of changing

01:12:59.820 --> 01:13:02.490
civil liberties
consciousness in that decade,

01:13:02.490 --> 01:13:06.540
where techniques that were
once deemed, you know,

01:13:06.540 --> 01:13:08.850
tolerable or acceptable, you know,

01:13:08.850 --> 01:13:12.090
you were really seeing a
different set of assessments

01:13:12.090 --> 01:13:15.000
about individual rights,
about civil liberties.

01:13:15.000 --> 01:13:17.490
And so a lot of Hoover's techniques,

01:13:17.490 --> 01:13:20.913
as well also came under a lot
more, a lot more scrutiny.

01:13:22.410 --> 01:13:26.490
- [Speaker] I was gonna ask
what sources gave you, like,

01:13:26.490 --> 01:13:29.610
it sounds like he was a
very political person,

01:13:29.610 --> 01:13:33.750
and I'm wondering where you
felt like you got the most frank

01:13:33.750 --> 01:13:36.810
and honest access to his
voice and his thinking

01:13:36.810 --> 01:13:40.203
and his mind in correspondence
or anything else?

01:13:42.030 --> 01:13:44.640
- Well, I think there are a
couple of different things

01:13:44.640 --> 01:13:45.510
that come to mind.

01:13:45.510 --> 01:13:49.830
So one is, are the FBI files themselves,

01:13:49.830 --> 01:13:52.080
which as I said, have all
sorts of problems, right?

01:13:52.080 --> 01:13:56.070
They're police files,
and so they have certain,

01:13:56.070 --> 01:13:58.590
you know, obvious biases, et cetera.

01:13:58.590 --> 01:14:01.173
They also are often redacted,

01:14:02.010 --> 01:14:05.100
so you can't find out
everything that's in them.

01:14:05.100 --> 01:14:09.240
My personal favorite file on
that front is the FBI's file

01:14:09.240 --> 01:14:13.140
on the CIA, which, you
know, has page after page.

01:14:13.140 --> 01:14:15.510
where they say, you know, Dear Director,

01:14:15.510 --> 01:14:17.130
the most interesting thing happened today,

01:14:17.130 --> 01:14:18.930
and then it's all just black, right?

01:14:18.930 --> 01:14:20.220
So you can't actually have access

01:14:20.220 --> 01:14:23.223
to any page after page of redaction.

01:14:24.810 --> 01:14:27.120
But the advantage of those FBI files

01:14:27.120 --> 01:14:28.710
is that during Hoover's lifetime,

01:14:28.710 --> 01:14:31.800
he believed that no one
else would ever have access.

01:14:31.800 --> 01:14:34.140
There was no Freedom of Information Act.

01:14:34.140 --> 01:14:36.480
There were no congressional
intelligence committees.

01:14:36.480 --> 01:14:40.110
And in fact, there was no
mechanism aside from the courts,

01:14:40.110 --> 01:14:42.120
which occasionally cost, you know,

01:14:42.120 --> 01:14:44.403
forced the FBI to cough something up.

01:14:45.450 --> 01:14:48.330
Hoover believed he had total
control over those files,

01:14:48.330 --> 01:14:51.930
and therefore, they're
pretty candid in these files,

01:14:51.930 --> 01:14:55.170
both about, you know,
when you read their files

01:14:55.170 --> 01:14:58.020
on Martin Luther King and what
they're plotting against him,

01:14:58.020 --> 01:15:00.270
and they just write it all down.

01:15:00.270 --> 01:15:02.880
And that's true in many, many instances.

01:15:02.880 --> 01:15:05.040
And then Hoover himself,

01:15:05.040 --> 01:15:07.560
had a habit of writing
things in the margins.

01:15:07.560 --> 01:15:09.300
So he had a blue pen,

01:15:09.300 --> 01:15:10.710
he could write things in the margins,

01:15:10.710 --> 01:15:15.017
and so he's just scrawling
all of these often, you know,

01:15:16.110 --> 01:15:20.640
kind of epithets and his
true opinions of many people.

01:15:20.640 --> 01:15:23.580
So those I actually think
are pretty direct access.

01:15:23.580 --> 01:15:27.990
And then the most direct and
literal access to his voice

01:15:27.990 --> 01:15:30.060
are the Johnson and Nixon tapes,

01:15:30.060 --> 01:15:31.320
which are really interesting

01:15:31.320 --> 01:15:34.050
because he had very close
relationships with both of them.

01:15:34.050 --> 01:15:37.470
Lots of their phone
conversations are recorded

01:15:37.470 --> 01:15:40.830
and you listen to them
and they are talking

01:15:40.830 --> 01:15:42.900
about a vast array of things.

01:15:42.900 --> 01:15:46.920
And yeah, and they're often pretty frank

01:15:46.920 --> 01:15:48.303
with each other in those.

01:15:49.590 --> 01:15:51.450
- [Speaker] I'm not
sure if you're familiar

01:15:51.450 --> 01:15:53.700
with the organization
that's called The Family,

01:15:53.700 --> 01:15:56.040
that there's been a lot written
about it over the years.

01:15:56.040 --> 01:15:58.860
And you know, they claim
that Supreme Court justices

01:15:58.860 --> 01:15:59.940
and senators.

01:15:59.940 --> 01:16:02.580
In your research, did
you find any connection

01:16:02.580 --> 01:16:05.103
between Hoover and The Family?

01:16:06.780 --> 01:16:08.460
- I didn't find direct connections,

01:16:08.460 --> 01:16:11.278
and I'm sort of generally familiar

01:16:11.278 --> 01:16:15.390
with The Family without
knowing all of the specifics.

01:16:15.390 --> 01:16:19.680
But Hoover's relationship with the right,

01:16:19.680 --> 01:16:22.560
and with right wing
organizations and with, you know,

01:16:22.560 --> 01:16:24.930
a certain kind of nascent religious right

01:16:24.930 --> 01:16:27.030
that's beginning to
emerge during his lifetime

01:16:27.030 --> 01:16:28.953
was really interesting to me.

01:16:30.240 --> 01:16:33.060
Though, a lot of the kind
of conservative movement

01:16:33.060 --> 01:16:36.450
that starts in the '50s and into the '60s

01:16:36.450 --> 01:16:39.333
kind of takes aim at
federal bureaucrats, right,

01:16:40.468 --> 01:16:41.910
as the worst people in the world,

01:16:41.910 --> 01:16:44.250
is anti-statist in lots of ways, right?

01:16:44.250 --> 01:16:49.250
A kind of Buckley-ite critique
of the federal government.

01:16:49.380 --> 01:16:52.470
Hoover was always the one federal servant,

01:16:52.470 --> 01:16:55.740
lifetime career bureaucrat
that they loved,

01:16:55.740 --> 01:16:58.410
and one of the places that this emerges

01:16:58.410 --> 01:17:01.140
pretty dramatically is with
the John Birch Society, right,

01:17:01.140 --> 01:17:03.250
which in many ways is kind of a forerunner

01:17:04.290 --> 01:17:07.320
of the sorts of organizations
that you're talking about.

01:17:07.320 --> 01:17:11.370
John Birch Society, far
right, kind of grassroots,

01:17:11.370 --> 01:17:15.870
anti-communist organization in
the late '50s and early '60s.

01:17:15.870 --> 01:17:19.233
And the John Birch Society
loves Hoover, right.

01:17:19.233 --> 01:17:21.990
They are constantly assigning his books.

01:17:21.990 --> 01:17:23.880
They're sort of saying, you
know, the great authority,

01:17:23.880 --> 01:17:25.500
J, Edgar Hoover says, you know,

01:17:25.500 --> 01:17:27.870
the Communist menace is here
and it will never go away,

01:17:27.870 --> 01:17:30.030
and we all have to participate in this.

01:17:30.030 --> 01:17:33.210
But Hoover himself was
actually pretty skeptical

01:17:33.210 --> 01:17:35.730
of groups like the John Birch Society

01:17:35.730 --> 01:17:39.090
because he thought they were extremists,

01:17:39.090 --> 01:17:40.860
they were a little out of control,

01:17:40.860 --> 01:17:42.720
and so there's a real ambivalence there.

01:17:42.720 --> 01:17:45.390
So, you know, I think
that that would be sort of

01:17:45.390 --> 01:17:50.070
his relationship with an
organization like The Family.

01:17:50.070 --> 01:17:51.870
The only other thing that I would say

01:17:51.870 --> 01:17:56.040
is that Hoover was himself, you know,

01:17:56.040 --> 01:17:58.680
I think he considered himself

01:17:58.680 --> 01:18:01.620
a kind of deeply religious Christian.

01:18:01.620 --> 01:18:05.880
He didn't go to church all that
often, from what I can tell,

01:18:05.880 --> 01:18:09.690
but he spoke often,
particularly in the 1950s,

01:18:09.690 --> 01:18:13.530
about the need for religiosity.

01:18:13.530 --> 01:18:17.340
He had, you know, send all
your children to Sunday school.

01:18:17.340 --> 01:18:19.440
He had been a Sunday school teacher,

01:18:19.440 --> 01:18:21.390
and I think institutionally,

01:18:21.390 --> 01:18:24.630
he then begins to bring a
lot of that into the FBI.

01:18:24.630 --> 01:18:28.350
So he begins to hire Mormons
in much greater numbers

01:18:28.350 --> 01:18:31.890
and Catholics in much greater
numbers in the '50s and '60s,

01:18:31.890 --> 01:18:35.190
and he begins to bring a set
of kind of religious rituals,

01:18:35.190 --> 01:18:40.190
communion breakfasts, prayer
breakfasts into the FBI itself.

01:18:40.410 --> 01:18:44.670
So there are ways in which
he's doing some of that similar

01:18:44.670 --> 01:18:48.960
kind of work within the Bureau itself.

01:18:48.960 --> 01:18:53.730
- [Speaker] So it sounds
like with the religious part

01:18:53.730 --> 01:18:57.303
of his life, lukewarm as maybe it was,

01:18:58.560 --> 01:19:02.850
there's that dichotomy again,
between what he is portraying

01:19:02.850 --> 01:19:04.800
or what he's advocating for, right,

01:19:04.800 --> 01:19:06.360
and what he's really living.

01:19:06.360 --> 01:19:10.200
Same thing with, "If I Had
a Son", was that the book?

01:19:10.200 --> 01:19:14.040
Looking at his photo
album and his writings,

01:19:14.040 --> 01:19:19.040
the marginalia that you
surely pored over for years,

01:19:19.260 --> 01:19:21.990
what is your sense of
where he was mentally

01:19:21.990 --> 01:19:23.190
with that dichotomy?

01:19:23.190 --> 01:19:27.150
Is it, this is self aggrandizement
for career advancement,

01:19:27.150 --> 01:19:30.480
or he could, he was
skillfully enough to occupy

01:19:30.480 --> 01:19:32.220
both spheres and do it, you know,

01:19:32.220 --> 01:19:34.380
to his bureaucratic success where,

01:19:34.380 --> 01:19:36.720
I mean, how did he square that?

01:19:36.720 --> 01:19:39.270
- That's a great question,
and I'm not sure I found

01:19:39.270 --> 01:19:41.760
the final answer to that question.

01:19:41.760 --> 01:19:44.423
- Got lucid.
- Right, right.

01:19:44.423 --> 01:19:48.330
There's one of my favorite
discoveries that helped me

01:19:48.330 --> 01:19:53.330
get a little sense of that
maybe is that in 1950,

01:19:53.520 --> 01:19:57.510
he was on vacation with
Clyde Tolson in Los Angeles,

01:19:57.510 --> 01:20:01.200
and a reporter started
following them around.

01:20:01.200 --> 01:20:03.570
They didn't see the
reporter, but you know,

01:20:03.570 --> 01:20:05.550
they went to the farmer's market together.

01:20:05.550 --> 01:20:08.790
They went and got ice
cream cones together,

01:20:08.790 --> 01:20:10.890
and then they went to a bookstore,

01:20:10.890 --> 01:20:13.200
and this reporter was following them,

01:20:13.200 --> 01:20:16.980
and the reporter looked at the
books that they were buying,

01:20:16.980 --> 01:20:19.530
and Tolson was buying
a couple of westerns,

01:20:19.530 --> 01:20:21.540
you know, beach reading.

01:20:21.540 --> 01:20:26.190
But Hoover was buying two
books by a psychoanalyst

01:20:26.190 --> 01:20:31.050
named Karen Horney, who
was a famous psychoanalyst

01:20:31.050 --> 01:20:32.850
of that period, wrote popular books.

01:20:32.850 --> 01:20:35.790
Her papers are actually here at Yale,

01:20:35.790 --> 01:20:40.530
and one was called, "Our Inner Conflicts,"

01:20:40.530 --> 01:20:44.280
and the other was called, "Self-Analysis."

01:20:44.280 --> 01:20:46.734
And so I was like, I'm
gonna read these books,

01:20:46.734 --> 01:20:48.600
and you know, the Yale
libraries being what they are,

01:20:48.600 --> 01:20:50.760
there were Karen Horney's books.

01:20:50.760 --> 01:20:55.350
And so in particular,
"Our Inner Conflicts"

01:20:55.350 --> 01:20:58.170
was about precisely what you're saying,

01:20:58.170 --> 01:21:01.740
what it does when your public image

01:21:01.740 --> 01:21:06.663
is so radically different
from the self that you know,

01:21:07.736 --> 01:21:09.180
and what that does to someone,

01:21:09.180 --> 01:21:12.120
to have that kind of radical difference.

01:21:12.120 --> 01:21:15.930
You're one thing in public,
you're another thing in private,

01:21:15.930 --> 01:21:18.330
and you have to live with that.

01:21:18.330 --> 01:21:22.230
And the premise of that
book was that it produced

01:21:22.230 --> 01:21:27.230
a certain kind of grandiosity,
a certain sort of rigidity,

01:21:27.570 --> 01:21:32.570
and a kind of hyper policing
of your public image

01:21:33.180 --> 01:21:34.950
and of those around you, right,

01:21:34.950 --> 01:21:38.100
a heightened need for control, et cetera.

01:21:38.100 --> 01:21:39.630
And it was kind of remarkable.

01:21:39.630 --> 01:21:42.150
So I don't know if we can
take this and therefore,

01:21:42.150 --> 01:21:43.950
apply it directly to Hoover,

01:21:43.950 --> 01:21:45.720
although there were a
lot of features of that

01:21:45.720 --> 01:21:48.780
that did resonate with who he was.

01:21:48.780 --> 01:21:51.150
But what was interesting
to me is that it seemed

01:21:51.150 --> 01:21:54.900
that he himself, was
actually trying to sort out

01:21:54.900 --> 01:21:56.190
that question, right,

01:21:56.190 --> 01:21:58.770
in ways that he couldn't
speak about publicly.

01:21:58.770 --> 01:22:01.080
Maybe he spoke about with friends,

01:22:01.080 --> 01:22:03.750
maybe it had to do with his sexuality.

01:22:03.750 --> 01:22:05.340
I mean, some of it must have had to do

01:22:05.340 --> 01:22:09.990
with his sexuality, but there
were other aspects of his life

01:22:09.990 --> 01:22:11.790
where that applied as well.

01:22:11.790 --> 01:22:14.580
So all I can say is that, you know,

01:22:14.580 --> 01:22:17.370
there are glimmers that he
was asking those questions,

01:22:17.370 --> 01:22:18.230
even if they never quite got reconciled.

01:22:20.670 --> 01:22:24.040
- [Speaker] I think it's
fascinating that the

01:22:26.070 --> 01:22:27.510
concept of the deep state

01:22:27.510 --> 01:22:30.840
is living with us to become new,

01:22:30.840 --> 01:22:35.840
and it seems like that
his role in creating

01:22:35.940 --> 01:22:37.980
what now people think
of as the deep state,

01:22:37.980 --> 01:22:41.700
was so, or what do you think
about his sort of ongoing

01:22:41.700 --> 01:22:45.780
legacy or both with the
nation and with the right?

01:22:45.780 --> 01:22:49.740
- Yeah, so one of the things
that's been really fascinating

01:22:49.740 --> 01:22:53.373
to me over the last five years,
really during the Trump era,

01:22:54.240 --> 01:22:59.240
is to see a kind of dramatic
reversal in what we got,

01:22:59.790 --> 01:23:01.740
really from the end of Hoover's life

01:23:01.740 --> 01:23:06.740
up until fairly recently,
which is politics in which,

01:23:08.139 --> 01:23:09.330
you know, in the wake of Hoover's death,

01:23:09.330 --> 01:23:12.720
and then the church committee
investigations of the 1970s,

01:23:12.720 --> 01:23:16.830
which were investigations
into the FBI, the CIA,

01:23:16.830 --> 01:23:19.470
that really produced some
of the knowledge we have

01:23:19.470 --> 01:23:20.950
about all of their abuses

01:23:21.960 --> 01:23:24.480
over the course of the
20th century, right?

01:23:24.480 --> 01:23:28.020
In that moment, coming out of the 1960s,

01:23:28.020 --> 01:23:33.020
you had a pretty consistent
set of political sentiments

01:23:33.270 --> 01:23:35.790
in which democrats, liberals, and leftists

01:23:35.790 --> 01:23:39.120
were much more critical
of the FBI in particular,

01:23:39.120 --> 01:23:41.100
than republicans, conservatives, right.

01:23:41.100 --> 01:23:44.340
Republicans and conservatives
tended to love the FBI,

01:23:44.340 --> 01:23:48.450
democrats, liberals, progressives
be very suspicious of it.

01:23:48.450 --> 01:23:51.360
And today that has
flipped very dramatically

01:23:51.360 --> 01:23:54.600
and some of that simply
has to do with, you know,

01:23:54.600 --> 01:23:57.720
the Trump era investigations themselves,

01:23:57.720 --> 01:24:01.470
I think has to do with,
you know, a broader sense

01:24:01.470 --> 01:24:06.300
that the part of the FBI that
is in fact about, you know,

01:24:06.300 --> 01:24:11.300
fact finding and expertise
and law enforcement is itself,

01:24:12.210 --> 01:24:14.820
worth protecting in a
period when, you know,

01:24:14.820 --> 01:24:17.403
facts seem to be up for debate.

01:24:18.660 --> 01:24:20.820
But, so now we have, as you're saying,

01:24:20.820 --> 01:24:22.500
a situation in which, you know,

01:24:22.500 --> 01:24:25.770
a lot of the most vocal
deep state critiques

01:24:25.770 --> 01:24:28.110
are coming from the right,
and I think you can see

01:24:28.110 --> 01:24:30.330
a little bit of that
during Hoover's years.

01:24:30.330 --> 01:24:33.060
I mean, particularly among
white supremacist groups

01:24:33.060 --> 01:24:36.210
in the white South, there's
always a lot of resistance

01:24:36.210 --> 01:24:39.540
to federal authority, to the FBI.

01:24:39.540 --> 01:24:40.620
And so I think you can see

01:24:40.620 --> 01:24:42.450
some of those strains coming out now.

01:24:42.450 --> 01:24:43.860
And of course, there's always been

01:24:43.860 --> 01:24:47.910
a sort of libertarian
critique of the security state

01:24:47.910 --> 01:24:50.223
of federal surveillance, the deep state.

01:24:52.484 --> 01:24:54.330
But, but yeah, it seems
very consistent now.

01:24:54.330 --> 01:24:59.310
There's no question Hoover
is an architect of that,

01:24:59.310 --> 01:25:02.460
and some of that politics
come out of his era, you know,

01:25:02.460 --> 01:25:05.280
James Comey, blurbed
the book, read the book,

01:25:05.280 --> 01:25:07.800
and is someone that I've
had a few conversations with

01:25:07.800 --> 01:25:12.153
over the years about Hoover and, you know,

01:25:13.110 --> 01:25:15.870
both looking at him from the outside

01:25:15.870 --> 01:25:18.000
and then just speaking
with him a little bit

01:25:18.000 --> 01:25:21.390
about these issues, you
know, I do think that the FBI

01:25:21.390 --> 01:25:25.410
has also often been in a
very difficult position,

01:25:25.410 --> 01:25:28.920
where it's situated in
the executive branch.

01:25:28.920 --> 01:25:32.370
It is supposed to be
nonpartisan, fact-finding right,

01:25:32.370 --> 01:25:36.360
outside of politics, and yet
again and again in its history,

01:25:36.360 --> 01:25:39.540
it gets dragged into these
incredibly politicized

01:25:39.540 --> 01:25:43.140
investigations, incredibly
partisan situations,

01:25:43.140 --> 01:25:45.060
where it's legitimately quite difficult

01:25:45.060 --> 01:25:47.190
to figure out what to do, right.

01:25:47.190 --> 01:25:50.310
Hoover had that experience quite often,

01:25:50.310 --> 01:25:54.420
especially in the '60s around
the major assassinations,

01:25:54.420 --> 01:25:57.213
later on for the FBI around Watergate.

01:25:58.260 --> 01:26:02.460
And I think you can see
exactly that structural tension

01:26:02.460 --> 01:26:04.950
at play right now, and it
so happens that, you know,

01:26:04.950 --> 01:26:08.250
it's playing out in a very
particular partisan way

01:26:08.250 --> 01:26:09.083
at the moment.

01:26:09.083 --> 01:26:13.680
But yes, defund the FBI is
not coming from the left

01:26:13.680 --> 01:26:14.610
in this moment, right?

01:26:14.610 --> 01:26:16.833
It is a right wing slogan.

01:26:18.450 --> 01:26:21.453
- [Speaker] He was in power,
you said, for 48 years.

01:26:22.350 --> 01:26:27.257
How do you think he maintained
that power so consistently?

01:26:29.160 --> 01:26:33.570
And number two is, you
mentioned President Kennedy

01:26:33.570 --> 01:26:35.580
wanting to maybe fire him.

01:26:35.580 --> 01:26:37.113
Why do you think he did not?

01:26:38.790 --> 01:26:42.780
- Well, I think those questions
are related to each other.

01:26:42.780 --> 01:26:45.540
And I think the easy answer,

01:26:45.540 --> 01:26:47.910
and probably the most popular answer

01:26:47.910 --> 01:26:50.160
to how Hoover stayed in power for so long

01:26:50.160 --> 01:26:51.900
was that he had the
goods on people, right,

01:26:51.900 --> 01:26:56.900
that he had files on lots of
important people in the media,

01:26:57.120 --> 01:26:59.820
in politics, and the
White House, in Congress,

01:26:59.820 --> 01:27:02.040
and there is, actually,
there is some truth to that.

01:27:02.040 --> 01:27:05.640
And it's certainly the
case with John Kennedy,

01:27:05.640 --> 01:27:10.640
that the FBI knew an awful
lot about Kennedy's sex life.

01:27:11.460 --> 01:27:13.830
They knew an awful lot about, you know,

01:27:13.830 --> 01:27:18.360
kind overt action decisions
that the Kennedys had made.

01:27:18.360 --> 01:27:19.770
They knew, for instance,

01:27:19.770 --> 01:27:22.440
that John Kennedy shared a girlfriend

01:27:22.440 --> 01:27:25.230
with the head of the Chicago mob, right?

01:27:25.230 --> 01:27:28.650
So pretty, pretty compromising stuff.

01:27:28.650 --> 01:27:31.350
And Kennedy knew that the FBI knew that.

01:27:31.350 --> 01:27:35.730
And so, you know, I think that's
at least part of the story,

01:27:35.730 --> 01:27:37.500
particularly in Hoover's later years,

01:27:37.500 --> 01:27:40.470
when he has such a concentration of power.

01:27:40.470 --> 01:27:43.530
But, you know, one of
the things that I wanted

01:27:43.530 --> 01:27:44.940
to really bring out in the book

01:27:44.940 --> 01:27:47.010
is that I don't think you
can understand his power

01:27:47.010 --> 01:27:50.220
simply as this form of coercion,

01:27:50.220 --> 01:27:54.270
because he had lots of support as well,

01:27:54.270 --> 01:27:56.190
over the course of his career,

01:27:56.190 --> 01:27:59.490
and he was incredibly skilled
at building that support.

01:27:59.490 --> 01:28:02.880
So the main reason that many
presidents didn't fire him

01:28:02.880 --> 01:28:05.850
is that he was really,
really useful to them, right?

01:28:05.850 --> 01:28:09.660
He would do their bidding,
he would keep their secrets.

01:28:09.660 --> 01:28:11.850
He was occasionally, you know,

01:28:11.850 --> 01:28:15.630
resistant in ways that
they didn't appreciate,

01:28:15.630 --> 01:28:18.930
but often was incredibly accommodating

01:28:18.930 --> 01:28:22.080
to White House wanted done.

01:28:22.080 --> 01:28:24.720
So he had control over his bureaucracy,

01:28:24.720 --> 01:28:28.860
he did what presidents wanted,
and they saw value in him.

01:28:28.860 --> 01:28:31.920
But he was also really good at, you know,

01:28:31.920 --> 01:28:34.440
creating a popular constituency.

01:28:34.440 --> 01:28:37.800
The FBI built up relationships
with organizations

01:28:37.800 --> 01:28:39.720
like the American Legion,

01:28:39.720 --> 01:28:42.180
Daughters of the American Revolution,

01:28:42.180 --> 01:28:45.780
and they sort of, anytime
anyone critiqued the FBI,

01:28:45.780 --> 01:28:49.410
you know, every American Legion
Post would sort of rise up

01:28:49.410 --> 01:28:52.890
in protest about our
great heroic FBI director.

01:28:52.890 --> 01:28:54.330
So he was very good at that.

01:28:54.330 --> 01:28:59.040
He had a huge amount of energy
devoted to public relations

01:28:59.040 --> 01:29:01.740
and to press relations in particular.

01:29:01.740 --> 01:29:03.990
So had lots of columnists in his pocket

01:29:03.990 --> 01:29:06.510
who would also rise to his defense.

01:29:06.510 --> 01:29:07.890
And as I suggested,

01:29:07.890 --> 01:29:09.540
in talking about congressional committees,

01:29:09.540 --> 01:29:12.900
he was really smart
about building a series

01:29:12.900 --> 01:29:16.410
of kind of mutually
beneficial relationships

01:29:16.410 --> 01:29:19.680
with congressmen and
senators in particular,

01:29:19.680 --> 01:29:23.310
a lot of whom really liked
him and really supported him.

01:29:23.310 --> 01:29:28.310
So, to go back to Kennedy,
but I think this is indicative

01:29:28.950 --> 01:29:32.610
of his power more broadly,

01:29:32.610 --> 01:29:35.100
Kennedy did not like him personally.

01:29:35.100 --> 01:29:36.450
They didn't get along that well,

01:29:36.450 --> 01:29:40.380
but Kennedy knew that Hoover
had a lot of information,

01:29:40.380 --> 01:29:42.840
the ability to get that information out,

01:29:42.840 --> 01:29:46.230
and Kennedy also says
that he actually thinks

01:29:46.230 --> 01:29:49.380
that J. Hoover is A, more popular,

01:29:49.380 --> 01:29:54.210
and B, has a more loyal
political constituency

01:29:54.210 --> 01:29:55.710
than Kennedy himself does.

01:29:55.710 --> 01:29:59.040
And so, you know, they thought
Hoover was more powerful

01:29:59.040 --> 01:30:01.180
and more popular and not a place

01:30:02.687 --> 01:30:05.465
to waste a lot of political capital.

01:30:05.465 --> 01:30:07.298
- [Speaker] Thank you.

01:30:08.255 --> 01:30:10.766
- Okay and with that,
another round of applause.

01:30:10.766 --> 01:30:13.016
(applause)

01:30:13.931 --> 01:30:16.514
(bright music)

